{"timestamp":"2026-10-27T17:29:02.524Z","ordinal":419353,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"function_call_output","id":"fco_01a17e68-893c-7b42-a19e-78d355bb463b","call_id":"call_c2233f3164c346be9bda9d2c102e6023","output":"{\"agents\":[{\"agent_name\":\"/root\",\"agent_status\":\"running\"},{\"agent_name\":\"/root/current_main_rc_path_audit\",\"agent_status\":\"running\"},{\"agent_name\":\"/root/heldout_public_coverage_audit\",\"agent_status\":{\"completed\":\"Since `2026-10-26T12:18:17Z`, Issue #1 gained coordinator comments #6415806863 and #6423073471. The issue was last updated at `2026-10-26T17:37:53Z`; all 22 comments are from the coordinator, with no custodian response or handoff. The claims search also shows no active custodian/author claim.\\n\\nAt current `origin/main@303ea322a7148b4a9d1a8231d7ce1c8f824bbae4`, the evaluator still rejects legacy `hidden.command` for qualification (`eval/validate.mjs:49`); the README still requires custodian-led conversion or replacement, repeat custody checks, and a new manifest identity. The `2026-10-27T05:30:30Z` release-loop record reports RC10 published but still lists `blackbox_v1_custodian_sealing` as a blocker.\\n\\nNo private material was accessed.\"}},{\"agent_name\":\"/root/plan05_readonly_review\",\"agent_status\":{\"completed\":\"**CHANGES_REQUIRED** — exact PR #277 head `54d0f9f5be930a782f4a44abea15d015be6c4d3f`, base `ceabfccf466ab39fc80c274400bae5aa0744a227`. Diff SHA-256: `adab08087707c5b94fc4fd05a3ff3c98b35e912b6f736dc671ca77066a8917bf`.\\n\\n- **[P1] The hook-name scan leaves a config-change race.** [git_hook_guard.ts](/home/mike/nexus-code/cli/src/core/git_hook_guard.ts:21) discovers current event-hook names, then [lines 41–45](/home/mike/nexus-code/cli/src/core/git_hook_guard.ts:41) disables only those names. The guarded Git operation runs afterward in a separate process. A workspace-controlled config include added or changed between the scan and that operation can introduce a new hook name that remains enabled. The regression proves suppression for hooks present before discovery, but not this race ([checkpoint test](/home/mike/nexus-code/cli/test/checkpoint_git_hook.test.ts:117), [retention test](/home/mike/nexus-code/cli/test/retention_git_hook.test.ts:30)). Git documents `hook.<event>.enabled=false` as disabling all hooks for an event; applying that override for each requested event would avoid name discovery and close this gap ([Git config documentation](https://git-scm.com/docs/git-config)).\\n\\n- **[P2] Retention hides guard failures as successful deletion.** [retention.ts](/home/mike/nexus-code/cli/src/core/retention.ts:1103) removes the checkpoint record before the guarded ref deletion. Errors from the new guard or `update-ref` are swallowed at [lines 1111–1129](/home/mike/nexus-code/cli/src/core/retention.ts:1111), after which [lines 1132–1137](/home/mike/nexus-code/cli/src/core/retention.ts:1132) count the item as deleted. A malformed inherited Git config can therefore leave an orphan ref while retention reports success. The evidence README discloses this residual, but the execution result should surface the partial failure.\\n\\nThe helper’s Git-config parsing and precedence approach is otherwise coherent; the included-config and inherited-`GIT_CONFIG_PARAMETERS` tests exercise the static cases. I did not run tests, as requested.\"}}]}","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a17e33-4871-7821-919e-a68cb04742bc","create_time":1793122142.5244849}},"metadata":{"client_authored":false,"fallback_token_limit_override":12000}}
