{"timestamp":"2026-10-27T17:53:14.007Z","ordinal":420405,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a14cb3-25b0-7883-b3a1-0fa7cc559f92","turn_id":"01a17e71-922f-7ef3-a2c1-bcdcbd1f8423","item":{"type":"Extension","kind":"web.search","id":"exec-54dd9022-289b-4d2b-88b7-906215e83ef2","query":"site:git-scm.com/docs/config hook.<hookname>.enabled hook.<hookname>.event command Git config hook disable all ...","action":{"type":"search","query":null,"queries":["site:git-scm.com/docs/config hook.<hookname>.enabled hook.<hookname>.event command Git config hook disable all","site:git-scm.com/docs/git-config hook event enabled Git 2.54 configured hooks"]},"results":[{"type":"text_result","domain":"git-scm.com","ref_id":"turn106search0","snippet":"git config remove-section [<file-option>] <name> ... This is usually only advisable for expert users and on a per-command basis using configuration parameters of the form","title":"Git - git-config Documentation","url":"https://git-scm.com/docs/git-config"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"git-scm.com","ref_id":"turn106search1","snippet":"Commands are run in the order Git encounters their associated `hook.`<friendly-name>`.event` configs during the configuration parse (see git-config[1]). ... git config set hook.<some-name>.command <pa","title":"Git - git-hook Documentation","url":"https://git-scm.com/docs/git-hook.html"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"git-scm.com","ref_id":"turn106search2","snippet":"The hook events that trigger the command are configured with `hook.`<friendly-name>`.event`. ... See git-hook[1]. ... hook.<friendly-name>.enabled ... Set to `false` to disable the hook without","title":"Git - git-hook Documentation","url":"https://git-scm.com/docs/git-hook/2.54.0.html"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"git-scm.com","ref_id":"turn106search3","snippet":"By default the hooks directory is `$GIT_DIR/hooks`, but that can be changed via the `core.hooksPath` configuration variable (see git-config[1]). ... The hook always runs after","title":"Git - githooks Documentation","url":"https://git-scm.com/docs/githooks"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"git-scm.com","ref_id":"turn106search4","snippet":"This setting has no effect unless all configured hooks for the event have `hook.`<friendly-name>`.parallel` set to `true`. ... ## GIT","title":"Git - git-hook Documentation","url":"https://git-scm.com/docs/git-hook"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"git-scm.com","ref_id":"turn106search5","snippet":"Some configuration options and hook files may cause Git to run arbitrary shell commands. ... See the entry for `safe.directory` in git-config[1].","title":"Git - git Documentation","url":"https://git-scm.com/docs/git"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"git-scm.com","ref_id":"turn106search6","snippet":"By default the hooks directory is `$GIT_DIR/hooks`, but that can be changed via the `core.hooksPath` configuration variable (see git-config[1]).","title":"Git - githooks Documentation","url":"https://git-scm.com/docs/githooks/2.16.6"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"git-scm.com","ref_id":"turn106search7","snippet":"By default the hooks directory is `$GIT_DIR/hooks`, but that can be changed via the `core.hooksPath` configuration variable (see git-config[1]).","title":"Git - githooks Documentation","url":"https://git-scm.com/docs/githooks/2.46.0.html"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"git-scm.com","ref_id":"turn106search8","snippet":"By default the hooks directory is `$GIT_DIR/hooks`, but that can be changed via the `core.hooksPath` configuration variable (see git-config[1]).","title":"Git - githooks Documentation","url":"https://git-scm.com/docs/githooks/2.36.0"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"git-scm.com","ref_id":"turn106search9","snippet":"You can also disable all hooks entirely by setting `core.hooksPath` to `/dev/null`. This is usually only advisable for expert users and on a per-command basis","title":"Git - git-config Documentation","url":"https://git-scm.com/docs/git-config/2.54.0.html"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"git-scm.com","ref_id":"turn106search10","snippet":"You can also disable all hooks entirely by setting `core.hooksPath` to `/dev/null`. This is usually only advisable for expert users and on a per-command basis","title":"Git - git-config Documentation","url":"https://git-scm.com/docs/git-config/2.50.0"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"git-scm.com","ref_id":"turn106search11","snippet":"You can also disable all hooks entirely by setting `core.hooksPath` to `/dev/null`. This is usually only advisable for expert users and on a per-command basis","title":"Git - git-config Documentation","url":"https://git-scm.com/docs/git-config/2.51.1.html"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"git-scm.com","ref_id":"turn106search12","snippet":"- `git config user.name 'Your Name'` ... - `git config --global ...","title":"Git Cheat Sheet","url":"https://git-scm.com/cheat-sheet.pdf"}]},"started_at_ms":1793123592833,"completed_at_ms":1793123594007}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-27T17:53:15.030Z","ordinal":420406,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a17e7e-b316-76b0-821e-24e4253c9d0a","call_id":"call_93bbd504d9bf42d18894aacb937c6959","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 2.2 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"Git - git-config Documentation (https://git-scm.com/docs/git-config)\nciteturn106search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday;     git config remove-section [<file-option>] <name> ... This is usually only advisable for expert users and on a per-command basis using configuration parameters of the form `git` `-c` `core.hooksPath=/dev/null` .... ... Switch to enable or disable all hooks for the <event> hook event.\nIts default value is `$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/git/attributes`. If `$XDG_CONFIG_HOME` is either not set or empty, `$HOME/.config/git/attributes` is used instead.\n\ncore.hooksPath\n    \n\nBy default Git will look for your hooks in the `$GIT_DIR/hooks` directory. Set this to different path, e.g. `/etc/git/hooks`, and Git will try to find your hooks in that directory, e.g. `/etc/git/hooks/pre-receive` instead of in `$GIT_DIR/hooks/pre-receive`.\n\nThe path can be either absolute or relative. A relative path is taken as relative to the directory where the hooks are run (see the \"DESCRIPTION\" section of githooks[5]).\n\nThis configuration variable is useful in cases where you’d like to centrally configure your Git hooks instead of configuring them on a per-repository basis, or as a more flexible and centralized alternative to having an `init.templateDir` where you’ve changed default hooks.\n\nYou can also disable all hooks entirely by setting `core.hooksPath` to `/dev/null`. This is usually only advisable for expert users and on a per-command basis using configuration parameters of the form `git` `-c` `core.hooksPath=/dev/null` ....\n\ncore.editor\n    \n\nCommands such as `commit` and `tag` that let you edit messages by launching an editor use the value of this variable when it is set, and the environment variable `GIT_EDITOR` is not set. See git-var[1].\n\nhook.<friendly-name>.command\n    \n\nThe command to execute for `hook.`<friendly-name>. <friendly-name> is a unique name that identifies this hook. The hook events that trigger the command are configured with `hook.`<friendly-name>`.event`. The value can be an executable path or a shell oneliner. If more than one value is specified for the same <friendly-name>, only the last value parsed is used. See git-hook[1].\n\nhook.<friendly-name>.event\n    \n\nThe hook events that trigger `hook.`<friendly-name>. The value is the name of a hook event, like \"pre-commit\" or \"update\". (See githooks[5] for a complete list of hook events.) On the specified event, the associated `hook.`<friendly-name>`.command` is executed. This is a multi-valued key. To run `hook.`<friendly-name> on multiple events, specify the key more than once. An empty value resets the list of events, clearing any previously defined events for `hook.`<friendly-name>. See git-hook[1].\n\nThe <friendly-name> must not be the same as a known hook event name (e.g. do not use `hook.pre-commit.event`). Using a known event name as a friendly-name is a fatal error because it creates an ambiguity with `hook.`<event>`.enabled` and `hook.`<event>`.jobs`. For unknown event names, a warning is issued when <friendly-name> matches the event value.\n\nhook.<friendly-name>.enabled\n    \n\nWhether the hook `hook.`<friendly-name> is enabled. Defaults to `true`. Set to `false` to disable the hook without removing its configuration. This is particularly useful when a hook is defined in a system or global config file and needs to be disabled for a specific repository. See git-hook[1].\n\nhook.<friendly-name>.parallel\n    \n\nWhether the hook `hook.`<friendly-name> may run in parallel with other hooks for the same event. Defaults to `false`. Set to `true` only when the hook script is safe to run concurrently with other hooks for the same event. If any hook for an event does not have this set to `true`, all hooks for that event run sequentially regardless of `hook.jobs`. Only configured (named) hooks need to declare this. Traditional hooks found in the hooks directory do not need to, and run in parallel when the effective job count is greater than 1. See git-hook[1].\n\nhook.<event>.enabled\n    \n\nSwitch to enable or disable all hooks for the <event> hook event. When set to `false`, no hooks fire for that event, regardless of any per-hook `hook.`<friendly-name>`.enabled` settings. Defaults to `true`. See git-hook[1].\n\nNote on naming: <event> must be the event name (e.g. `pre-commit`), not a hook friendly-name. Since using a known event name as a friendly-name is disallowed (see `hook.`<friendly-name>`.event` above), there is no ambiguity between event-level and per-hook `.enabled` settings for known events. For unknown events, if a friendly-name matches the event name despite the warning, `.enabled` is treated as per-hook only.\n\nhook.<event>.jobs\n    \n\nSpecifies how many hooks can be run simultaneously for the <event> hook event (e.g. `hook.post-receive.jobs` `=` `4`). Overrides `hook.jobs` for this specific event. The same parallelism restrictions apply: this setting has no effect unless all configured hooks for the event have `hook.`<friendly-name>`.parallel` set to `true`. Set to `-1` to use the number of available CPU cores. Must be a positive integer or `-1`; zero is rejected with a warning. See git-hook[1].\n\nNote on naming: although this key resembles `hook.`<friendly-name>`.*` (a per-hook setting), <event> must be the event name, not a hook friendly name. The key component is stored literally and looked up by event name at runtime with no translation between the two namespaces. A key like `hook.my-hook.jobs` is stored under `\"my-hook\"` but the lookup at runtime uses the event name (e.g. `\"post-receive\"`), so `hook.my-hook.jobs` is silently ignored even when `my-hook` is registered for that event. Use `hook.post-receive.jobs` or any other valid event name when setting `hook.`<event>`.jobs`.\n\nhook.jobs\n    \n\nSpecifies how many hooks can be run simultaneously during parallelized hook execution. If unspecified, defaults to 1 (serial execution). Set to `-1` to use the number of available CPU cores. Can be overridden on a per-event basis with `hook.`<event>`.jobs`. Some hooks always run sequentially regardless of this setting because they operate on shared data and cannot safely be parallelized:\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGit - git-hook Documentation (https://git-scm.com/docs/git-hook.html)\nciteturn106search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Commands are run in the order Git encounters their associated `hook.`<friendly-name>`.event` configs during the configuration parse (see git-config[1]). ...     git config set hook.<some-name>.command <path-to-script> ... Switch to enable or disable all hooks for the <event> hook event.\n    \n    [hook \"linter\"]\n      event = pre-commit\n      event = pre-push\n      command = ~/bin/linter --cpp20\n\nWith this config, `~/bin/linter` `--cpp20` would be run by Git before a commit is generated (during `pre-commit`) as well as before a push is performed (during `pre-push`).\n\nAnd if you wanted to run your linter as well as a secret-leak detector during only the \"pre-commit\" hook event, you would configure it instead like so:\n    \n    [hook \"linter\"]\n      event = pre-commit\n      command = ~/bin/linter --cpp20\n    [hook \"no-leaks\"]\n      event = pre-commit\n      command = ~/bin/leak-detector\n\nWith this config, before a commit is generated (during `pre-commit`), Git would first start `~/bin/linter` `--cpp20` and second start `~/bin/leak-detector`. It would evaluate the output of each when deciding whether to proceed with the commit.\n\nFor a full list of hook events which you can set your `hook.`<friendly-name>`.event` to, and how hooks are invoked during those events, see githooks[5].\n\nGit will ignore any `hook.`<friendly-name>`.event` that specifies an event it doesn’t recognize. This is intended so that tools which wrap Git can use the hook infrastructure to run their own hooks; see \"WRAPPERS\" for more guidance.\n\nIn general, when instructions suggest adding a script to `.git/hooks/`<hook-event>, you can specify it in the config instead by running:\n    \n    git config set hook.<some-name>.command <path-to-script>\n    git config set --append hook.<some-name>.event <hook-event>\n\nThis way you can share the script between multiple repos. That is, `cp` `~/my-script.sh` `~/project/.git/hooks/pre-commit` would become:\n    \n    git config set hook.my-script.command ~/my-script.sh\n    git config set --append hook.my-script.event pre-commit\n\nIf Git begins to use a hook event named the same thing as your wrapper hook, it may invoke your users' hooks in unintended and unsupported ways.\n\n## CONFIGURATION\n\nhook.<friendly-name>.command\n    \n\nThe command to execute for `hook.`<friendly-name>. <friendly-name> is a unique name that identifies this hook. The hook events that trigger the command are configured with `hook.`<friendly-name>`.event`. The value can be an executable path or a shell oneliner. If more than one value is specified for the same <friendly-name>, only the last value parsed is used.\n\nhook.<friendly-name>.event\n    \n\nThe hook events that trigger `hook.`<friendly-name>. The value is the name of a hook event, like \"pre-commit\" or \"update\". (See githooks[5] for a complete list of hook events.) On the specified event, the associated `hook.`<friendly-name>`.command` is executed. This is a multi-valued key. To run `hook.`<friendly-name> on multiple events, specify the key more than once. An empty value resets the list of events, clearing any previously defined events for `hook.`<friendly-name>.\n\nThe <friendly-name> must not be the same as a known hook event name (e.g. do not use `hook.pre-commit.event`). Using a known event name as a friendly-name is a fatal error because it creates an ambiguity with `hook.`<event>`.enabled` and `hook.`<event>`.jobs`. For unknown event names, a warning is issued when <friendly-name> matches the event value.\n\nhook.<friendly-name>.enabled\n    \n\nWhether the hook `hook.`<friendly-name> is enabled. Defaults to `true`. Set to `false` to disable the hook without removing its configuration. This is particularly useful when a hook is defined in a system or global config file and needs to be disabled for a specific repository.\n\nhook.<friendly-name>.parallel\n    \n\nWhether the hook `hook.`<friendly-name> may run in parallel with other hooks for the same event. Defaults to `false`. Set to `true` only when the hook script is safe to run concurrently with other hooks for the same event. If any hook for an event does not have this set to `true`, all hooks for that event run sequentially regardless of `hook.jobs`. Only configured (named) hooks need to declare this. Traditional hooks found in the hooks directory do not need to, and run in parallel when the effective job count is greater than 1.\n\nhook.<event>.enabled\n    \n\nSwitch to enable or disable all hooks for the <event> hook event. When set to `false`, no hooks fire for that event, regardless of any per-hook `hook.`<friendly-name>`.enabled` settings. Defaults to `true`.\n\nNote on naming: <event> must be the event name (e.g. `pre-commit`), not a hook friendly-name. Since using a known event name as a friendly-name is disallowed (see `hook.`<friendly-name>`.event` above), there is no ambiguity between event-level and per-hook `.enabled` settings for known events. For unknown events, if a friendly-name matches the event name despite the warning, `.enabled` is treated as per-hook only.\n\nhook.<event>.jobs\n    \n\nSpecifies how many hooks can be run simultaneously for the <event> hook event (e.g. `hook.post-receive.jobs` `=` `4`). Overrides `hook.jobs` for this specific event. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGit - git-hook Documentation (https://git-scm.com/docs/git-hook/2.54.0.html)\nciteturn106search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; Crawled: last month; The hook events that trigger the command are configured with `hook.`<friendly-name>`.event`. ... See git-hook[1]. ... hook.<friendly-name>.enabled ... Set to `false` to disable the hook without removing its configuration.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGit - githooks Documentation (https://git-scm.com/docs/githooks)\nciteturn106search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; By default the hooks directory is `$GIT_DIR/hooks`, but that can be changed via the `core.hooksPath` configuration variable (see git-config[1]). ... The hook always runs after the automatic note copying (see \"notes.rewrite.<command>\" in git-config[1]) has happened, and thus has access to these notes.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGit - git-hook Documentation (https://git-scm.com/docs/git-hook)\nciteturn106search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; This setting has no effect unless all configured hooks for the event have `hook.`<friendly-name>`.parallel` set to `true`. ... ## GIT\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGit - git Documentation (https://git-scm.com/docs/git)\nciteturn106search5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Some configuration options and hook files may cause Git to run arbitrary shell commands. ... See the entry for `safe.directory` in git-config[1].\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGit - githooks Documentation (https://git-scm.com/docs/githooks/2.16.6)\nciteturn106search6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; By default the hooks directory is `$GIT_DIR/hooks`, but that can be changed via the `core.hooksPath` configuration variable (see git-config[1]).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGit - githooks Documentation (https://git-scm.com/docs/githooks/2.46.0.html)\nciteturn106search7 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.2 years ago; Crawled: last month; By default the hooks directory is `$GIT_DIR/hooks`, but that can be changed via the `core.hooksPath` configuration variable (see git-config[1]).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGit - githooks Documentation (https://git-scm.com/docs/githooks/2.36.0)\nciteturn106search8 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.5 years ago; Crawled: yesterday; By default the hooks directory is `$GIT_DIR/hooks`, but that can be changed via the `core.hooksPath` configuration variable (see git-config[1]).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGit - git-config Documentation (https://git-scm.com/docs/git-config/2.54.0.html)\nciteturn106search9 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; You can also disable all hooks entirely by setting `core.hooksPath` to `/dev/null`. This is usually only advisable for expert users and on a per-command basis using configuration parameters of the form `git` `-c` `core.hooksPath=/dev/null` ....\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGit - git-config Documentation (https://git-scm.com/docs/git-config/2.50.0)\nciteturn106search10 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.4 years ago; Crawled: yesterday; You can also disable all hooks entirely by setting `core.hooksPath` to `/dev/null`. This is usually only advisable for expert users and on a per-command basis using configuration parameters of the form `git` `-c` `core.hooksPath=/dev/null` .... ... If you plan to use your config with older and newer versions of Git, you may want to specify both:\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGit - git-config Documentation (https://git-scm.com/docs/git-config/2.51.1.html)\nciteturn106search11 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; Crawled: last month; You can also disable all hooks entirely by setting `core.hooksPath` to `/dev/null`. This is usually only advisable for expert users and on a per-command basis using configuration parameters of the form `git` `-c` `core.hooksPath=/dev/null` ....\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGit Cheat Sheet (https://git-scm.com/cheat-sheet.pdf)\nciteturn106search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11 months ago;     - `git config user.name 'Your Name'` ...     - `git config --global ...\n"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a17e71-922f-7ef3-a2c1-bcdcbd1f8423","create_time":1793123595.0306835}},"metadata":{"client_authored":false,"fallback_token_limit_override":12000}}
