{"timestamp":"2026-10-27T18:04:29.371Z","ordinal":421031,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a14cb3-25b0-7883-b3a1-0fa7cc559f92","turn_id":"01a17e71-922f-7ef3-a2c1-bcdcbd1f8423","item":{"type":"Extension","kind":"web.search","id":"exec-a70dfced-a537-4473-92df-cb4bc1136dca","query":"site:git-scm.com/docs/config hook.<name>.enabled event Git 2.54 ...","action":{"type":"search","query":null,"queries":["site:git-scm.com/docs/config hook.<name>.enabled event Git 2.54","site:git-scm.com/docs/githooks repository config hook event hook.enabled"]},"results":[{"type":"text_result","domain":"git-scm.com","ref_id":"turn108search0","snippet":"For \"list\"; prefix each configured hook’s friendly name with a tab-separated config scope (e.g. `local`, `global`, `system`), mirroring the output style of `git` `config` `--show-scope`.","title":"Git - git-hook Documentation","url":"https://git-scm.com/docs/git-hook/2.54.0.html"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"git-scm.com","ref_id":"turn108search1","snippet":"The files `.git/config` and optionally `config.worktree` (see the \"CONFIGURATION FILE\" section of git-worktree[1]) in each repository are used to store the configuration for that repository,","title":"Git - git-config Documentation","url":"https://git-scm.com/docs/git-config"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"git-scm.com","ref_id":"turn108search2","snippet":"2.54.0 2026-04-20 ... Commands are run in the order Git encounters their associated `hook.`<friendly-name>`.event` configs during the configuration parse (see git-config[1]). ... Since using a","title":"Git - git-hook Documentation","url":"https://git-scm.com/docs/git-hook.html"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"git-scm.com","ref_id":"turn108search3","snippet":"When enabled, the default post-update hook runs `git` `update-server-info` to keep the information used by dumb transports (e.g., HTTP) up to date. ... To distinguish","title":"Git - githooks Documentation","url":"https://git-scm.com/docs/githooks"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"git-scm.com","ref_id":"turn108search4","snippet":"This setting has no effect unless all configured hooks for the event have `hook.`<friendly-name>`.parallel` set to `true`. ... ## GIT","title":"Git - git-hook Documentation","url":"https://git-scm.com/docs/git-hook"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"git-scm.com","ref_id":"turn108search5","snippet":"The maintenance strategy can be further tweaked by setting `maintenance.`<task>`.enabled` and `maintenance.`<task>`.schedule`. ... This boolean config option controls whether the maintenance task with","title":"Git - git-config Documentation","url":"https://git-scm.com/docs/git-config/2.54.0.html"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"git-scm.com","ref_id":"turn108search6","snippet":"Version 2.22.0 ▾ githooks last updated in 2.54.0 ... ## NAME ... By default the hooks directory is `$GIT_DIR/hooks`, but that can be changed via","title":"Git - githooks Documentation","url":"https://git-scm.com/docs/githooks/2.22.0"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"git-scm.com","ref_id":"turn108search7","snippet":"Some configuration options and hook files may cause Git to run arbitrary shell commands. ... See the entry for `safe.directory` in git-config[1].","title":"Git - git Documentation","url":"https://git-scm.com/docs/git"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"git-scm.com","ref_id":"turn108search8","snippet":"By default, the configuration flag `receive.denyNonFastForwards` is enabled in shared repositories, so that you cannot force a non fast-forwarding push into it. ... Files and","title":"Git - git-init Documentation","url":"https://git-scm.com/docs/git-init/2.54.0.html"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"git-scm.com","ref_id":"turn108search9","snippet":"This hook can be used to prevent forced update on certain refs by making sure that the object name is a commit object that is","title":"Git - githooks Documentation","url":"https://git-scm.com/docs/githooks/2.16.6"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"git-scm.com","ref_id":"turn108search10","snippet":"Its default value is `$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/git/attributes`. ... core.hooksPath","title":"Git - git-config Documentation","url":"https://git-scm.com/docs/git-config/2.50.0"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"git-scm.com","ref_id":"turn108search11","snippet":"2.54.0 2026-04-20 ... git hook run [--ignore-missing] <hook-name> [-- <hook-args>]","title":"Git - git-hook Documentation","url":"https://git-scm.com/docs/git-hook/2.36.0.html"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"git-scm.com","ref_id":"turn108search12","snippet":"- `git config user.name 'Your Name'`","title":"Git Cheat Sheet","url":"https://git-scm.com/cheat-sheet.pdf"}]},"started_at_ms":1793124268478,"completed_at_ms":1793124269370}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-27T18:04:30.442Z","ordinal":421032,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a17e89-016a-7010-a6a1-6c3612501792","call_id":"call_d86737ec65ae4d2f9678dc9531173c22","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 2.0 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"Git - git-hook Documentation (https://git-scm.com/docs/git-hook/2.54.0.html)\nciteturn108search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; Crawled: last month; For \"list\"; prefix each configured hook’s friendly name with a tab-separated config scope (e.g. `local`, `global`, `system`), mirroring the output style of `git` `config` `--show-scope`. ... Take care to name your wrapper’s hook events in a way which is unlikely to overlap with Git’s native hooks (see githooks[5]) - a hook event named `mywrappertool-validate-commit` is much less likely to be added to native Git than a hook event named `validate-commit`.\n## CONFIGURATION\n\nhook.<friendly-name>.command\n    \n\nThe command to execute for `hook.`<friendly-name>. <friendly-name> is a unique name that identifies this hook. The hook events that trigger the command are configured with `hook.`<friendly-name>`.event`. The value can be an executable path or a shell oneliner. If more than one value is specified for the same <friendly-name>, only the last value parsed is used. See git-hook[1].\n\nhook.<friendly-name>.event\n    \n\nThe hook events that trigger `hook.`<friendly-name>. The value is the name of a hook event, like \"pre-commit\" or \"update\". (See githooks[5] for a complete list of hook events.) On the specified event, the associated `hook.`<friendly-name>`.command` is executed. This is a multi-valued key. To run `hook.`<friendly-name> on multiple events, specify the key more than once. An empty value resets the list of events, clearing any previously defined events for `hook.`<friendly-name>. See git-hook[1].\n\nhook.<friendly-name>.enabled\n    \n\nWhether the hook `hook.`<friendly-name> is enabled. Defaults to `true`. Set to `false` to disable the hook without removing its configuration. This is particularly useful when a hook is defined in a system or global config file and needs to be disabled for a specific repository. See git-hook[1].\n\n## SEE ALSO\n\ngithooks[5]\n\n## GIT\n\nPart of the git[1] suite--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGit - git-config Documentation (https://git-scm.com/docs/git-config)\nciteturn108search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; The files `.git/config` and optionally `config.worktree` (see the \"CONFIGURATION FILE\" section of git-worktree[1]) in each repository are used to store the configuration for that repository, and `$HOME/.gitconfig` is used to store a per-user configuration as fallback values for the `.git/config` file. ... Switch to enable or disable all hooks for the <event> hook event. ... Since using a known event name as a friendly-name is disallowed (see `hook.`<friendly-name>`.event` above), there is no ambiguity between event-level and per-hook `.enabled` settings for known events.\nIts default value is `$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/git/attributes`. If `$XDG_CONFIG_HOME` is either not set or empty, `$HOME/.config/git/attributes` is used instead.\n\ncore.hooksPath\n    \n\nBy default Git will look for your hooks in the `$GIT_DIR/hooks` directory. Set this to different path, e.g. `/etc/git/hooks`, and Git will try to find your hooks in that directory, e.g. `/etc/git/hooks/pre-receive` instead of in `$GIT_DIR/hooks/pre-receive`.\n\nThe path can be either absolute or relative. A relative path is taken as relative to the directory where the hooks are run (see the \"DESCRIPTION\" section of githooks[5]).\n\nThis configuration variable is useful in cases where you’d like to centrally configure your Git hooks instead of configuring them on a per-repository basis, or as a more flexible and centralized alternative to having an `init.templateDir` where you’ve changed default hooks.\n\nYou can also disable all hooks entirely by setting `core.hooksPath` to `/dev/null`. This is usually only advisable for expert users and on a per-command basis using configuration parameters of the form `git` `-c` `core.hooksPath=/dev/null` ....\n\ncore.editor\n    \n\nCommands such as `commit` and `tag` that let you edit messages by launching an editor use the value of this variable when it is set, and the environment variable `GIT_EDITOR` is not set. See git-var[1].\n\nhook.<friendly-name>.command\n    \n\nThe command to execute for `hook.`<friendly-name>. <friendly-name> is a unique name that identifies this hook. The hook events that trigger the command are configured with `hook.`<friendly-name>`.event`. The value can be an executable path or a shell oneliner. If more than one value is specified for the same <friendly-name>, only the last value parsed is used. See git-hook[1].\n\nhook.<friendly-name>.event\n    \n\nThe hook events that trigger `hook.`<friendly-name>. The value is the name of a hook event, like \"pre-commit\" or \"update\". (See githooks[5] for a complete list of hook events.) On the specified event, the associated `hook.`<friendly-name>`.command` is executed. This is a multi-valued key. To run `hook.`<friendly-name> on multiple events, specify the key more than once. An empty value resets the list of events, clearing any previously defined events for `hook.`<friendly-name>. See git-hook[1].\n\nThe <friendly-name> must not be the same as a known hook event name (e.g. do not use `hook.pre-commit.event`). Using a known event name as a friendly-name is a fatal error because it creates an ambiguity with `hook.`<event>`.enabled` and `hook.`<event>`.jobs`. For unknown event names, a warning is issued when <friendly-name> matches the event value.\n\nhook.<friendly-name>.enabled\n    \n\nWhether the hook `hook.`<friendly-name> is enabled. Defaults to `true`. Set to `false` to disable the hook without removing its configuration. This is particularly useful when a hook is defined in a system or global config file and needs to be disabled for a specific repository. See git-hook[1].\n\nhook.<friendly-name>.parallel\n    \n\nWhether the hook `hook.`<friendly-name> may run in parallel with other hooks for the same event. Defaults to `false`. Set to `true` only when the hook script is safe to run concurrently with other hooks for the same event. If any hook for an event does not have this set to `true`, all hooks for that event run sequentially regardless of `hook.jobs`. Only configured (named) hooks need to declare this. Traditional hooks found in the hooks directory do not need to, and run in parallel when the effective job count is greater than 1. See git-hook[1].\n\nhook.<event>.enabled\n    \n\nSwitch to enable or disable all hooks for the <event> hook event. When set to `false`, no hooks fire for that event, regardless of any per-hook `hook.`<friendly-name>`.enabled` settings. Defaults to `true`. See git-hook[1].\n\nNote on naming: <event> must be the event name (e.g. `pre-commit`), not a hook friendly-name. Since using a known event name as a friendly-name is disallowed (see `hook.`<friendly-name>`.event` above), there is no ambiguity between event-level and per-hook `.enabled` settings for known events. For unknown events, if a friendly-name matches the event name despite the warning, `.enabled` is treated as per-hook only.\n\nhook.<event>.jobs\n    \n\nSpecifies how many hooks can be run simultaneously for the <event> hook event (e.g. `hook.post-receive.jobs` `=` `4`). Overrides `hook.jobs` for this specific event. The same parallelism restrictions apply: this setting has no effect unless all configured hooks for the event have `hook.`<friendly-name>`.parallel` set to `true`. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGit - git-hook Documentation (https://git-scm.com/docs/git-hook.html)\nciteturn108search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; 2.54.0 2026-04-20 ... Commands are run in the order Git encounters their associated `hook.`<friendly-name>`.event` configs during the configuration parse (see git-config[1]). ... Since using a known event name as a friendly-name is disallowed (see `hook.`<friendly-name>`.event` above), there is no ambiguity between event-level and per-hook `.enabled` settings for known events.\nIf Git begins to use a hook event named the same thing as your wrapper hook, it may invoke your users' hooks in unintended and unsupported ways.\n\n## CONFIGURATION\n\nhook.<friendly-name>.command\n    \n\nThe command to execute for `hook.`<friendly-name>. <friendly-name> is a unique name that identifies this hook. The hook events that trigger the command are configured with `hook.`<friendly-name>`.event`. The value can be an executable path or a shell oneliner. If more than one value is specified for the same <friendly-name>, only the last value parsed is used.\n\nhook.<friendly-name>.event\n    \n\nThe hook events that trigger `hook.`<friendly-name>. The value is the name of a hook event, like \"pre-commit\" or \"update\". (See githooks[5] for a complete list of hook events.) On the specified event, the associated `hook.`<friendly-name>`.command` is executed. This is a multi-valued key. To run `hook.`<friendly-name> on multiple events, specify the key more than once. An empty value resets the list of events, clearing any previously defined events for `hook.`<friendly-name>.\n\nThe <friendly-name> must not be the same as a known hook event name (e.g. do not use `hook.pre-commit.event`). Using a known event name as a friendly-name is a fatal error because it creates an ambiguity with `hook.`<event>`.enabled` and `hook.`<event>`.jobs`. For unknown event names, a warning is issued when <friendly-name> matches the event value.\n\nhook.<friendly-name>.enabled\n    \n\nWhether the hook `hook.`<friendly-name> is enabled. Defaults to `true`. Set to `false` to disable the hook without removing its configuration. This is particularly useful when a hook is defined in a system or global config file and needs to be disabled for a specific repository.\n\nhook.<friendly-name>.parallel\n    \n\nWhether the hook `hook.`<friendly-name> may run in parallel with other hooks for the same event. Defaults to `false`. Set to `true` only when the hook script is safe to run concurrently with other hooks for the same event. If any hook for an event does not have this set to `true`, all hooks for that event run sequentially regardless of `hook.jobs`. Only configured (named) hooks need to declare this. Traditional hooks found in the hooks directory do not need to, and run in parallel when the effective job count is greater than 1.\n\nhook.<event>.enabled\n    \n\nSwitch to enable or disable all hooks for the <event> hook event. When set to `false`, no hooks fire for that event, regardless of any per-hook `hook.`<friendly-name>`.enabled` settings. Defaults to `true`.\n\nNote on naming: <event> must be the event name (e.g. `pre-commit`), not a hook friendly-name. Since using a known event name as a friendly-name is disallowed (see `hook.`<friendly-name>`.event` above), there is no ambiguity between event-level and per-hook `.enabled` settings for known events. For unknown events, if a friendly-name matches the event name despite the warning, `.enabled` is treated as per-hook only.\n\nhook.<event>.jobs\n    \n\nSpecifies how many hooks can be run simultaneously for the <event> hook event (e.g. `hook.post-receive.jobs` `=` `4`). Overrides `hook.jobs` for this specific event. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGit - githooks Documentation (https://git-scm.com/docs/githooks)\nciteturn108search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; When enabled, the default post-update hook runs `git` `update-server-info` to keep the information used by dumb transports (e.g., HTTP) up to date. ... To distinguish these cases, you can inspect the current value of <ref-name> via `git` `rev-parse`. ... The hook always runs after the automatic note copying (see \"notes.rewrite.<command>\" in git-config[1]) has happened, and thus has access to these notes.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGit - git-hook Documentation (https://git-scm.com/docs/git-hook)\nciteturn108search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; This setting has no effect unless all configured hooks for the event have `hook.`<friendly-name>`.parallel` set to `true`. ... ## GIT\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGit - git-config Documentation (https://git-scm.com/docs/git-config/2.54.0.html)\nciteturn108search5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; The maintenance strategy can be further tweaked by setting `maintenance.`<task>`.enabled` and `maintenance.`<task>`.schedule`. ... This boolean config option controls whether the maintenance task with name <task> is run when no `--task` option is specified to `git` `maintenance` `run`.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGit - githooks Documentation (https://git-scm.com/docs/githooks/2.22.0)\nciteturn108search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7.4 years ago; Crawled: yesterday; Version 2.22.0 ▾ githooks last updated in 2.54.0 ... ## NAME ... By default the hooks directory is `$GIT_DIR/hooks`, but that can be changed via the `core.hooksPath` configuration variable (see git-config[1]).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGit - git Documentation (https://git-scm.com/docs/git)\nciteturn108search7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Some configuration options and hook files may cause Git to run arbitrary shell commands. ... See the entry for `safe.directory` in git-config[1].\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGit - git-init Documentation (https://git-scm.com/docs/git-init/2.54.0.html)\nciteturn108search8 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; Crawled: 4 weeks ago; By default, the configuration flag `receive.denyNonFastForwards` is enabled in shared repositories, so that you cannot force a non fast-forwarding push into it. ... Files and directories in the template directory whose name do not start with a dot will be copied to the `$GIT_DIR` after it is created. ... The default template directory includes some directory structure, suggested \"exclude patterns\" (see gitignore[5]), and sample hook files. ... Both the command line option and the `GIT_DEFAULT_HASH` environment variable take precedence over this config.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGit - githooks Documentation (https://git-scm.com/docs/githooks/2.16.6)\nciteturn108search9 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; This hook can be used to prevent forced update on certain refs by making sure that the object name is a commit object that is a descendant of the commit object named by the old object name. ... When enabled, the default post-update hook runs git update-server-info to keep the information used by dumb transports (e.g., HTTP) up to date. If you are publishing a Git repository that is accessible via HTTP, you should probably enable this hook.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGit - git-config Documentation (https://git-scm.com/docs/git-config/2.50.0)\nciteturn108search10 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.4 years ago; Crawled: yesterday; Its default value is `$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/git/attributes`. ... core.hooksPath\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGit - git-hook Documentation (https://git-scm.com/docs/git-hook/2.36.0.html)\nciteturn108search11 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.5 years ago; Crawled: last month; 2.54.0 2026-04-20 ...     git hook run [--ignore-missing] <hook-name> [-- <hook-args>]\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGit Cheat Sheet (https://git-scm.com/cheat-sheet.pdf)\nciteturn108search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11 months ago;     - `git config user.name 'Your Name'`\n"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a17e71-922f-7ef3-a2c1-bcdcbd1f8423","create_time":1793124270.442152}},"metadata":{"client_authored":false,"fallback_token_limit_override":12000}}
