ne `hook.linter.command` event is valid - Git uses","title":"Git - git-hook Documentation","url":"https://git-scm.com/docs/git-hook.html"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"mirror.yandex.ru","ref_id":"turn111search9","snippet":"See git-hook(1). ... Switch to enable or disable all hooks for the <event> hook event. ... Since using a known event name as a friendly-name","title":"git-config(1)","url":"https://mirror.yandex.ru/pub/software/scm/git/docs/git-config.html"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"man.freebsd.org","ref_id":"turn111search10","snippet":"guity between event-level and per-hook .enabled settings for known ... per-hook setting), <event> must be the event name, not a hook friendly","title":"git-hook(1)","url":"https://man.freebsd.org/cgi/man.cgi?apropos=0&manpath=Debian+14.0+unstable&query=git-hook&sektion=1"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"kernel.googlesource.com","ref_id":"turn111search11","snippet":"+ `hook.<friendly-name>`. {see-git-hook} ... +`hook.<event>.enabled` and `hook.<event>.jobs`. ... + Switch to enable or disable all hooks for the `<event>` hook event.","title":"Diff - 1ea786d14a1bb47399ce7b5f1e5885d65139b35f^2..1ea786d14a1bb47399ce7b5f1e5885d65139b35f - pub/scm/git/git.git - Git at Google","url":"https://kernel.googlesource.com/pub/scm/git/git.git/%2B/1ea786d14a1bb47399ce7b5f1e5885d65139b35f%5E2..1ea786d14a1bb47399ce7b5f1e5885d65139b35f/"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"www.fzi.de","ref_id":"turn111search12","snippet":"The git hook privilege should thus be merged onto the administrator privilege so that only Gitea users with administrative privileges can set up git hooks.","title":"FSA-2020-3 Authenticated Remote Code Execution in Gitea 1.12.6 and Gogs 0.12.2","url":"https://www.fzi.de/wp-content/uploads/2022/02/doku-gitea-gogs.pdf"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"www.foo.be","ref_id":"turn111search13","snippet":"Each hook script is named after the event with which it is associated. ... Depending on your version of Git and your operating system, the","title":"With those warnings behind us, hooks exist for very good reasons, and their use can be incredibly advantageous.","url":"https://www.foo.be/cours/dess-20132014/b/OReilly%20Version%20Control%20with%20GIT.pdf"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"www.eecs.umich.edu","ref_id":"turn111search14","snippet":"One feature of most version control systems is hooks. A hook is an automated script or tool that runs at various points in time. ...","title":"Advanced - Git 2","url":"https://www.eecs.umich.edu/courses/eecs201/fa2024/files/assignments/adv-git2.pdf"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"archidep.ch","ref_id":"turn111search15","snippet":"- What is a Git hook? ... - Hooks are not under version control","title":"Git Hooks · ArchiDep","url":"https://archidep.ch/pdf/ArchiDep%20601%20-%20Automated%20Deployment%20-%20Git%20Hooks%20-%20Subject.pdf"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"www.devopsschool.com","ref_id":"turn111search16","snippet":"Git has one function named as hooks. ( http://git-scm.com/book ) In my words, Hooks like event trigger","title":"Git hooks","url":"https://www.devopsschool.com/slide/storage/training_logos/REDACTED/logo_345_1649921641_git-hooks.pdf"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"en.wikipedia.org","ref_id":"turn111search17","snippet":"A command-line session showing repository creation, addition of a file, and remote synchronization ... These criteria eliminated every version-control system in use at the time,","title":"Git","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Git"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"en.wikipedia.org","ref_id":"turn111search18","snippet":"Subversion, for example, can be configured to handle EOLs differently according to the file type, whereas Perforce converts all text files according to a single,","title":"Comparison of version-control software","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Comparison_of_version-control_software"}]},"started_at_ms":1793124363774,"completed_at_ms":1793124364699}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-27T18:06:05.665Z","ordinal":421132,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a17e8a-7561-7541-88ea-de275c1475b6","call_id":"call_580827a163e245ef84f119a049cb7929","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 1.9 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"Git - git-config Documentation (https://git-scm.com/docs/git-config)\nciteturn111search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; See git-hook[1]. ... Switch to enable or disable all hooks for the <event> hook event. ... Since using a known event name as a friendly-name is disallowed (see `hook.`<friendly-name>`.event` above), there is no ambiguity between event-level and per-hook `.enabled` settings for known events. ... The same parallelism restrictions apply: this setting has no effect unless all configured hooks for the event have `hook.`<friendly-name>`.parallel` set to `true`.\nhook.<event>.enabled\n    \n\nSwitch to enable or disable all hooks for the <event> hook event. When set to `false`, no hooks fire for that event, regardless of any per-hook `hook.`<friendly-name>`.enabled` settings. Defaults to `true`. See git-hook[1].\n\nNote on naming: <event> must be the event name (e.g. `pre-commit`), not a hook friendly-name. Since using a known event name as a friendly-name is disallowed (see `hook.`<friendly-name>`.event` above), there is no ambiguity between event-level and per-hook `.enabled` settings for known events. For unknown events, if a friendly-name matches the event name despite the warning, `.enabled` is treated as per-hook only.\n\nhook.<event>.jobs\n    \n\nSpecifies how many hooks can be run simultaneously for the <event> hook event (e.g. `hook.post-receive.jobs` `=` `4`). Overrides `hook.jobs` for this specific event. The same parallelism restrictions apply: this setting has no effect unless all configured hooks for the event have `hook.`<friendly-name>`.parallel` set to `true`. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ngit-config(1) (https://kernel.org/pub/software/scm/git/docs/git-config.html)\nciteturn111search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; See git-hook(1). ... Since using a known event name as a friendly-name is disallowed (see `hook.`<friendly-name>`.event` above), there is no ambiguity between event-level and per-hook .`enabled` settings for known events. ... The same parallelism restrictions apply: this setting has no effect unless all configured hooks for the event have `hook.`<friendly-name>`.parallel` set to `true`.\nOnly configured (named) hooks need to declare this. Traditional hooks found in the hooks directory do not need to, and run in parallel when the effective job count is greater than 1. See git-hook(1).\n\nhook.<event>.enabled\n    \n\nSwitch to enable or disable all hooks for the <event> hook event. When set to `false`, no hooks fire for that event, regardless of any per-hook `hook.`<friendly-name>`.enabled` settings. Defaults to `true`. See git-hook(1).\n\nNote on naming: <event> must be the event name (e.g. `pre-commit`), not a hook friendly-name. Since using a known event name as a friendly-name is disallowed (see `hook.`<friendly-name>`.event` above), there is no ambiguity between event-level and per-hook .`enabled` settings for known events. For unknown events, if a friendly-name matches the event name despite the warning, .`enabled` is treated as per-hook only.\n\nhook.<event>.jobs\n    \n\nSpecifies how many hooks can be run simultaneously for the <event> hook event (e.g. `hook.post-receive.jobs` `=` `4`). --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ngit/hook.c at master · git/git · GitHub (https://github.com/git/git/blob/master/hook.c)\nciteturn111search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; \"`git config set advice.ignoredHook false`.\"), ... * friendly-names are event-level switches (hook.<event>.enabled = false). ... /* to enable oneliners, let config-specified hooks run in shell.\n}\n\n}\n\n/* Populate `cache` with the complete hook configuration */\n\nstatic void build_hook_config_map(struct repository *r, struct strmap *cache)\n\n{\n\nstruct hook_all_config_cb cb_data = { 0 };\n\nstruct hashmap_iter iter;\n\nstruct strmap_entry *e;\n\nstrmap_init(&cb_data.commands);\n\nstrmap_init(&cb_data.event_hooks);\n\nstring_list_init_dup(&cb_data.disabled_hooks);\n\nstrmap_init(&cb_data.parallel_hooks);\n\nstrmap_init(&cb_data.event_jobs);\n\n/* Parse all configs in one run, capturing hook.* including hook.jobs. */\n\nrepo_config(r, hook_config_lookup_all, &cb_data);\n\nwarn_jobs_on_friendly_names(&cb_data);\n\n/*\n\n* Populate disabled_events: names in disabled_hooks that are not\n\n* friendly-names are event-level switches (hook.<event>.enabled = false).\n\n* Names that are friendly-names are already handled per-hook via the\n\n* hook_config_cache_entry.disabled flag below.\n\n*/\n\nif (r) {\n\nstring_list_clear(&r->disabled_events, 0);\n\nstring_list_init_dup(&r->disabled_events);\n\nfor (size_t i = 0; i < cb_data.disabled_hooks.nr; i++) {\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ngit-config(1) (https://www.kernel.org/pub/software/scm/git/docs/git-config.html)\nciteturn111search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; See git-hook(1). ... Since using a known event name as a friendly-name is disallowed (see `hook.`<friendly-name>`.event` above), there is no ambiguity between event-level and per-hook .`enabled` settings for known events. ... The same parallelism restrictions apply: this setting has no effect unless all configured hooks for the event have `hook.`<friendly-name>`.parallel` set to `true`.\nhook.<friendly-name>.enabled\n    \n\nWhether the hook `hook.`<friendly-name> is enabled. Defaults to `true`. Set to `false` to disable the hook without removing its configuration. This is particularly useful when a hook is defined in a system or global config file and needs to be disabled for a specific repository. See git-hook(1).\n\nhook.<friendly-name>.parallel\n    \n\nWhether the hook `hook.`<friendly-name> may run in parallel with other hooks for the same event. Defaults to `false`. Set to `true` only when the hook script is safe to run concurrently with other hooks for the same event. If any hook for an event does not have this set to `true`, all hooks for that event run sequentially regardless of `hook.jobs`. Only configured (named) hooks need to declare this. Traditional hooks found in the hooks directory do not need to, and run in parallel when the effective job count is greater than 1. See git-hook(1).\n\nhook.<event>.enabled\n    \n\nSwitch to enable or disable all hooks for the <event> hook event. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ngit-hook.html - external/gitster/git-htmldocs - Git at Google (https://chromium.googlesource.com/external/gitster/git-htmldocs/%2B/92515990e5be7c05b4cd06aa21ab1af3c57ca4d8/git-hook.html)\nciteturn111search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago;  | <p>Switch to enable or disable all hooks for the <em>&lt;event&gt;</em> hook event. ...  | there is no ambiguity between event-level and per-hook .<code>enabled</code> ...  | (a per-hook setting), <em>&lt;event&gt;</em> must be the event name, not a hook ...  | <h2 id=\"_git\">GIT</h2>\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ngit-config.html - external/gitster/git-htmldocs - Git at Google (https://chromium.googlesource.com/external/gitster/git-htmldocs/%2B/c531e17171f68e84a70d79a18bd0fabdd840e879/git-config.html)\nciteturn111search5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago;  | See <a href=\"git-hook.html\">git-hook(1)</a>.</p> ...  | there is no ambiguity between event-level and per-hook .<code>enabled</code> ...  | (a per-hook setting), <em>&lt;event&gt;</em> must be the event name, not a hook\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ngit/repository.h at master · git/git · GitHub (https://github.com/git/git/blob/master/repository.h)\nciteturn111search6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; /* Cached list of event names with hook.<event>.enabled = false. ... int repo_init(struct repository *r, const char *gitdir, const char *worktree);\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ngit-config.html - external/gitster/git-htmldocs - Git at Google (https://chromium.googlesource.com/external/gitster/git-htmldocs/%2B/748ad7c22ce356a99f766ba95392d2c07fef178b/git-config.html)\nciteturn111search7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; See <a href=\"git-hook.html\">git-hook(1)</a>.</p> ...  | <p>Switch to enable or disable all hooks for the <em>&lt;event&gt;</em> hook event. ...  | there is no ambiguity between event-level and per-hook .<code>enabled</code>\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGit - git-hook Documentation (https://git-scm.com/docs/git-hook.html)\nciteturn111search8 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Latest version ▾ git-hook last updated in 2.55.0 ... Although multiple `hook.linter.event` configs can be added, only one `hook.linter.command` event is valid - Git uses \"last-one-wins\" to determine which command to run.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ngit-config(1) (https://mirror.yandex.ru/pub/software/scm/git/docs/git-config.html)\nciteturn111search9 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; See git-hook(1). ... Switch to enable or disable all hooks for the <event> hook event. ... Since using a known event name as a friendly-name is disallowed (see `hook.`<friendly-name>`.event` above), there is no ambiguity between event-level and per-hook .`enabled` settings for known events.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ngit-hook(1) (https://man.freebsd.org/cgi/man.cgi?apropos=0&manpath=Debian+14.0+unstable&query=git-hook&sektion=1)\nciteturn111search10 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 months ago; Crawled: 2 months ago;     \t guity between event-level and\tper-hook  .enabled  settings  for  known ...     \t per-hook setting), <event> must be the event name, not a hook\tfriendly\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDiff - 1ea786d14a1bb47399ce7b5f1e5885d65139b35f^2..1ea786d14a1bb47399ce7b5f1e5885d65139b35f - pub/scm/git/git.git - Git at Google (https://kernel.googlesource.com/pub/scm/git/git.git/%2B/1ea786d14a1bb47399ce7b5f1e5885d65139b35f%5E2..1ea786d14a1bb47399ce7b5f1e5885d65139b35f/)\nciteturn111search11 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 months ago; Crawled: today;     +\t`hook.<friendly-name>`. {see-git-hook} ...     +`hook.<event>.enabled` and `hook.<event>.jobs`. ...     +\tSwitch to enable or disable all hooks for the `<event>` hook event.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFSA-2020-3 Authenticated Remote Code Execution in Gitea 1.12.6 and Gogs 0.12.2 (https://www.fzi.de/wp-content/uploads/2022/02/doku-gitea-gogs.pdf)\nciteturn111search12 [wordlim: 200] The git hook privilege should thus be merged onto the administrator privilege so that only Gitea users with administrative privileges can set up git hooks. ... ; If enabled all Gitea users with administrative privileges are able to perform ... ENABLE_GIT_HOOKS = false ... A workaround for Gitea instances that cannot be upgraded quickly is to disable git hooks in the config file by setting `DISABLE_GIT_HOOKS = true`. ... After notification, the Gitea team quickly created a Patch (PR: #11030) which was merged into Version 1.12.0.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nWith those warnings behind us, hooks exist for very good reasons, and their use can be incredibly advantageous. (https://www.foo.be/cours/dess-20132014/b/OReilly%20Version%20Control%20with%20GIT.pdf)\nciteturn111search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 17.4 years ago; Each hook script is named after the event with which it is associated. ... Depending on your version of Git and your operating system, the hooks are disabled either by removing the execute bit or by appending `.sample` to the hook\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAdvanced - Git 2 (https://www.eecs.umich.edu/courses/eecs201/fa2024/files/assignments/adv-git2.pdf)\nciteturn111search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.1 years ago; One feature of most version control systems is hooks. A hook is an automated script or tool that runs at various points in time. ... Git stores all of its information and configuration in a folder named `.git` in the root of each project. ... try adding lines like `echo $1` or `echo $(file $1)` or `echo $(cat $1)` to the hook and making commits, what happens?)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGit Hooks · ArchiDep (https://archidep.ch/pdf/ArchiDep%20601%20-%20Automated%20Deployment%20-%20Git%20Hooks%20-%20Subject.pdf)\nciteturn111search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9 months ago; - What is a Git hook? ...     - Hooks are not under version control\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGit hooks (https://www.devopsschool.com/slide/storage/training_logos/REDACTED/logo_345_1649921641_git-hooks.pdf)\nciteturn111search16 [wordlim: 200] Git has one function named as hooks. ( http://git-scm.com/book )\n\nIn my words, Hooks like event trigger\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGit (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Git)\nciteturn111search17 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 6 days ago; A command-line session showing repository creation, addition of a file, and remote synchronization ... These criteria eliminated every version-control system in use at the time, so immediately after the 2.6.12-rc2 Linux kernel development release, Torvalds set out to write his own. ... Event occurs at 00:01:30. ... *  4.4 Git on the Server - Setting Up the Server Archived 22 October 2014 at the Wayback Machine, Pro Git. ... You may remember when Git introduced a new version of its network fetch protocol way back in 2018.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nComparison of version-control software (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Comparison_of_version-control_software)\nciteturn111search18 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Subversion, for example, can be configured to handle EOLs differently according to the file type, whereas Perforce converts all text files according to a single, per-client setting. ...  Software  | Supports Git data format  | Atomic commits  | File renames  | Merge file renames  | Symbolic links  | Pre-/post-event hooks  | Signed revisions  | Merge tracking  | End of line conversions  | Tags  | International support  | Unicode filename support  | Supports large repos ... *  UTF-8 filenames are supported as of version 1.7.10 (MSysGit release notes). ... *  Integrity enabled change packages provide complete workflow and 21 CFR Part 11 compliant digital signatures against the item controlling the change package. ... *  It could be done via user level hooks\n"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a17e71-922f-7ef3-a2c1-bcdcbd1f8423","create_time":1793124365.665505}},"metadata":{"client_authored":false,"fallback_token_limit_override":12000}}
