{"timestamp":"2026-10-27T18:06:11.565Z","ordinal":421144,"type":"token_usage_record","payload":{"thread_id":"01a14cb3-25b0-7883-b3a1-0fa7cc559f92","turn_id":"01a17e71-922f-7ef3-a2c1-bcdcbd1f8423","session_id":"01a14cb3-25b0-7883-b3a1-0fa7cc559f92","root_turn_id":"01a17e71-922f-7ef3-a2c1-bcdcbd1f8423","response_id":"resp_060b64278a9dfb15016ae0e812731881a0be160544eb8aaae7","usage":{"input_tokens":111434,"cached_input_tokens":107136,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":61,"reasoning_output_tokens":11,"total_tokens":111495},"turn_token_usage":{"input_tokens":25543658,"cached_input_tokens":25298816,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":95351,"reasoning_output_tokens":55218,"total_tokens":25639009},"thread_token_usage":{"input_tokens":7751403550,"cached_input_tokens":7668751872,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":22168756,"reasoning_output_tokens":12832713,"total_tokens":7773572306}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-27T18:06:12.352Z","ordinal":421145,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a14cb3-25b0-7883-b3a1-0fa7cc559f92","turn_id":"01a17e71-922f-7ef3-a2c1-bcdcbd1f8423","item":{"type":"Extension","kind":"web.search","id":"exec-7d76d1a3-8254-4902-9222-d0ca36618d61","query":"'hook.<event>.enabled'","action":{"type":"findInPage","url":null,"pattern":"hook.<event>.enabled"},"results":[{"type":"text_result","domain":"git-scm.com","ref_id":"turn113view0","snippet":"Total lines: 5089","title":"Git - git-config Documentation","url":"https://git-scm.com/docs/git-config/2.55.0"}]},"started_at_ms":1793124371535,"completed_at_ms":1793124372352}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-27T18:06:13.421Z","ordinal":421146,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a17e8a-93ad-7290-be51-a0c02219696e","call_id":"call_f73b934da4b04aa9bf28b60917a33062","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 1.9 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"Git - git-config Documentation (https://git-scm.com/docs/git-config/2.55.0)\nciteturn113view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn112view0\",\"pattern\":\"hook.<event>.enabled\"}); Total lines: 5089\nL2982: Run the tool only if a diff is selected in the GUI. It guarantees that FILENAME is not empty.\nL2983: \nL2984: guitool.<name>.noConsole\nL2985: \nL2986: \nL2987: Run the command silently, without creating a window to display its output.\nL2988: \nL2989: guitool.<name>.noRescan\nL2990: \nL2991: \nL2992: Don’t rescan the working directory for changes after the tool finishes execution.\nL2993: \nL2994: guitool.<name>.confirm\nL2995: \nL2996: \nL2997: Show a confirmation dialog before actually running the tool.\nL2998: \nL2999: guitool.<name>.argPrompt\nL3000: Request a string argument from the user, and pass it to the tool through the `ARGS` environment variable. Since requesting an argument implies confirmation, the confirm option has no effect if this is enabled. If the option is set to true, yes, or 1, the dialog uses a built-in generic prompt; otherwise the exact value of the variable is used.\nL3001: \nL3002: guitool.<name>.revPrompt\nL3003: Request a single valid revision from the user, and set the `REVISION` environment variable. In other aspects this option is similar to argPrompt, and can be used together with it.\nL3004: \nL3005: guitool.<name>.revUnmerged\nL3006: \nL3007: \nL3008: Show only unmerged branches in the revPrompt subdialog. This is useful for tools similar to merge or rebase, but not for things like checkout or reset.\nL3009: \nL3010: guitool.<name>.title\nL3011: \nL3012: \nL3013: Specifies the title to use for the prompt dialog. The default is the tool name.\nL3014: \nL3015: guitool.<name>.prompt\nL3016: Specifies the general prompt string to display at the top of the dialog, before subsections for argPrompt and revPrompt. The default value includes the actual command.\nL3017: \nL3018: help.browser\nL3019: \nL3020: \nL3021: Specify the browser that will be used to display help in the web format. See 【38†git-help[1]】.\nL3022: \nL3023: help.format\nL3024: \nL3025: \nL3026: Override the default help format used by 【38†git-help[1]】. Values man, info, web and html are supported. man is the default. web and html are the same.\nL3027: \nL3028: help.autoCorrect\nL3029: If git detects typos and can identify exactly one valid command similar to the error, git will try to suggest the correct command or even run the suggestion automatically. Possible config values are:\nL3030: \nL3031:   * 0, \"false\", \"off\", \"no\", \"show\": show the suggested command (default).\nL3032: \nL3033:   * 1, \"true\", \"on\", \"yes\", \"immediate\": run the suggested command immediately.\nL3034: \nL3035:   * positive number > 1: run the suggested command after specified deciseconds (0.1 sec).\nL3036: \nL3037:   * \"never\": don’t run or show any suggested command.\nL3038:   * \"prompt\": show the suggestion and prompt for confirmation to run the command.\nL3039: \nL3040: help.htmlPath\nL3041: \nL3042: \nL3043: Specify the path where the HTML documentation resides. File system paths and URLs are supported. HTML pages will be prefixed with this path when help is displayed in the web format. This defaults to the documentation path of your Git installation.\nL3044: \nL3045: hook.<friendly-name>.command\nL3046: The command to execute for `hook.`<friendly-name>. <friendly-name> is a unique name that identifies this hook. The hook events that trigger the command are configured with `hook.`<friendly-name>`.event`. The value can be an executable path or a shell oneliner. If more than one value is specified for the same <friendly-name>, only the last value parsed is used. See 【265†git-hook[1]】.\nL3047: \nL3048: hook.<friendly-name>.event\nL3049: The hook events that trigger `hook.`<friendly-name>. The value is the name of a hook event, like \"pre-commit\" or \"update\". (See 【92†githooks[5]】 for a complete list of hook events.) On the specified event, the associated `hook.`<friendly-name>`.command` is executed. This is a multi-valued key. To run `hook.`<friendly-name> on multiple events, specify the key more than once. An empty value resets the list of events, clearing any previously defined events for `hook.`<friendly-name>. See 【265†git-hook[1]】.\nL3050: The <friendly-name> must not be the same as a known hook event name (e.g. do not use `hook.pre-commit.event`). Using a known event name as a friendly-name is a fatal error because it creates an ambiguity with `hook.`<event>`.enabled` and `hook.`<event>`.jobs`. For unknown event names, a warning is issued when <friendly-name> matches the event value.\nL3051: \nL3052: hook.<friendly-name>.enabled\nL3053: Whether the hook `hook.`<friendly-name> is enabled. Defaults to `true`. Set to `false` to disable the hook without removing its configuration. This is particularly useful when a hook is defined in a system or global config file and needs to be disabled for a specific repository. See 【265†git-hook[1]】.\nL3054: \nL3055: hook.<friendly-name>.parallel\nL3056: Whether the hook `hook.`<friendly-name> may run in parallel with other hooks for the same event. Defaults to `false`. Set to `true` only when the hook script is safe to run concurrently with other hooks for the same event. If any hook for an event does not have this set to `true`, all hooks for that event run sequentially regardless of `hook.jobs`. Only configured (named) hooks need to declare this.\nL3057: Traditional hooks found in the hooks directory do not need to, and run in parallel when the effective job count is greater than 1. See 【265†git-hook[1]】.\nL3058: hook.<event>.enabled\nL3059: \nL3060: \nL3061: Switch to enable or disable all hooks for the <event> hook event. When set to `false`, no hooks fire for that event, regardless of any per-hook `hook.`<friendly-name>`.enabled` settings. Defaults to `true`. See 【265†git-hook[1]】.\nL3062: Note on naming: <event> must be the event name (e.g. `pre-commit`), not a hook friendly-name. Since using a known event name as a friendly-name is disallowed (see `hook.`<friendly-name>`.event` above), there is no ambiguity between event-level and per-hook `.enabled` settings for known events. For unknown events, if a friendly-name matches the event name despite the warning, `.enabled` is treated as per-hook only.\nL3063: \nL3064: hook.<event>.jobs\nL3065: Specifies how many hooks can be run simultaneously for the <event> hook event (e.g. `hook.post-receive.jobs` `=` `4`). Overrides `hook.jobs` for this specific event. The same parallelism restrictions apply: this setting has no effect unless all configured hooks for the event have `hook.`<friendly-name>`.parallel` set to `true`. Set to `-1` to use the number of available CPU cores. Must be a positive integer or `-1`; zero is rejected with a warning. See 【265†git-hook[1]】.\nL3066: Note on naming: although this key resembles `hook.`<friendly-name>`.*` (a per-hook setting), <event> must be the event name, not a hook friendly name. The key component is stored literally and looked up by event name at runtime with no translation between the two namespaces. A key like `hook.my-hook.jobs` is stored under `\"my-hook\"` but the lookup at runtime uses the event name (e.g. `\"post-receive\"`), so `hook.my-hook.jobs` is silently ignored even when `my-hook` is registered for that event.\nL3067: Use `hook.post-receive.jobs` or any other valid event name when setting `hook.`<event>`.jobs`.\nL3068: hook.jobs\nL3069: \nL3070: \nL3071: Specifies how many hooks can be run simultaneously during parallelized hook execution. If unspecified, defaults to 1 (serial execution). Set to `-1` to use the number of available CPU cores. Can be overridden on a per-event basis with `hook.`<event>`.jobs`. Some hooks always run sequentially regardless of this setting because they operate on shared data and cannot safely be parallelized:\nL3072: \nL3073: `applypatch-msg`\nL3074: `prepare-commit-msg`\nL3075: `commit-msg`\nL3076: Receive a commit message file and may rewrite it in place.\nL3077: \nL3078: `pre-commit`\nL3079: `post-checkout`\nL3080: `push-to-checkout`\nL3081: `post-commit`\nL3082: \nL3083: \nL3084: Access the working tree, index, or repository state.\nL3085: \nL3086: This setting has no effect unless all configured hooks for the event have `hook.`<friendly-name>`.parallel` set to `true`.\nL3087: For `pre-push` hooks, which normally keep stdout and stderr separate, setting this to a value greater than 1 (or passing `-j`) will merge stdout into stderr to allow correct de-interleaving of parallel output.\nL3088: \nL3089: http.proxy\nL3090: Override the HTTP proxy, normally configured using the http_proxy, https_proxy, and all_proxy environment variables (see `curl`(`1`)). In addition to the syntax understood by curl, it is possible to specify a proxy string with a user name but no password, in which case git will attempt to acquire one in the same way it does for other credentials. See 【253†gitcredentials[7]】 for more information. The syntax thus is [REDACTED\nL3091: This can be overridden on a per-remote basis; see remote.<name>.proxy\nL3092: Any proxy, however configured, must be completely transparent and must not modify, transform, or buffer the request or response in any way. Proxies which are not completely transparent are known to cause various forms of breakage with Git.\nL3093: \nL3094: http.proxyAuthMethod\nL3095: Set the method with which to authenticate against the HTTP proxy. This only takes effect if the configured proxy string contains a user name part (i.e. is of the form user@host or user@host:port). This can be overridden on a per-remote basis; see `remote.`<name>`.proxyAuthMethod`. Both can be overridden by the `GIT_HTTP_PROXY_AUTHMETHOD` environment variable. Possible values are:\nL3096:   * `anyauth` - Automatically pick a suitable authentication method. It is assumed that the proxy answers an unauthenticated request with a 407 status code and one or more Proxy-authenticate headers with supported authentication methods. This is the default.\nL3097: \nL3098:   * `basic` - HTTP Basic authentication\nL3099: \nL3100:   * `digest` - HTTP Digest authentication; this prevents the password from being transmitted to the proxy in clear text\nL3101:   * `negotiate` - GSS-Negotiate authentication (compare the --negotiate option of `curl`(`1`))\nL3102: \nL3103:   * `ntlm` - NTLM authentication (compare the --ntlm option of `curl`(`1`))\nL3104: \nL3105: http.proxySSLCert\nL3106: \nL3107: \nL3108: The pathname of a file that stores a client certificate to use to authenticate with an HTTPS proxy. Can be overridden by the `GIT_PROXY_SSL_CERT` environment variable.\nL3109: \nL3110: http.proxySSLKey\nL3111: The pathname of a file that stores a private key to use to authenticate with an HTTPS proxy. Can be overridden by the `GIT_PROXY_SSL_KEY` environment variable.\nL3112: \nL3113: http.proxySSLCertPasswordProtected\nL3114: \nL3115: \nL3116: Enable Git’s password prompt for the proxy SSL certificate. Otherwise OpenSSL will prompt the user, possibly many times, if the certificate or private key is encrypted. Can be overridden by the `GIT_PROXY_SSL_CERT_PASSWORD_PROTECTED` environment variable.\nL3117: \nL3118: http.proxySSLCAInfo\nL3119: Pathname to the file containing the certificate bundle that should be used to verify the proxy with when using an HTTPS proxy. Can be overridden by the `GIT_PROXY_SSL_CAINFO` environment variable.\nL3120: \nL3121: http.emptyAuth\nL3122: \nL3123: \nL3124: Attempt authentication without seeking a username or password. This can be used to attempt GSS-Negotiate authentication without specifying a username in the URL, as libcurl normally requires a username for authentication. Possible values are:\nL3125:   * `auto` (default) - Send empty credentials only if the server’s 401 response advertises an authentication mechanism that requires them (such as GSS-Negotiate); otherwise fall back to prompting via the credential helper.\nL3126: \nL3127:   * `true` - Always send empty credentials on the very first request, before receiving any 401 response from the server.\nL3128: \nL3129:   * `false` - Never send empty credentials. Mechanisms that require empty credentials or an explicit username, such as GSS-Negotiate, will not work.\nL3130: http.proactiveAuth\nL3131: \nL3132: \nL3133: Attempt authentication without first making an unauthenticated attempt and receiving a 401 response. This can be used to ensure that all requests are authenticated. If `http.emptyAuth` is set to true, this value has no effect.\nL3134: If the credential helper used specifies an authentication scheme (i.e., via the `authtype` field), that value will be used; if a username and password is provided without a scheme, then Basic authentication is used. The value of the option determines the scheme requested from the helper. Possible values are:\nL3135: \nL3136:   * `basic` - Request Basic authentication from the helper.\nL3137: \nL3138:   * `auto` - Allow the helper to pick an appropriate scheme.\nL3139: \nL3140:   * `none` - Disable proactive authentication.\nL3141: Note that TLS should always be used with this configuration, since otherwise it is easy to accidentally expose plaintext credentials if Basic authentication is selected.\nL3142: \nL3143: http.delegation\nL3144: \nL3145: \nL3146: Control GSSAPI credential delegation. The delegation is disabled by default in libcurl since version 7.21.7. Set parameter to tell the server what it is allowed to delegate when it comes to user credentials. Used with GSS/kerberos. Possible values are:\nL3147: \nL3148:   * `none` - Don’t allow any delegation.\nL3149:   * `policy` - Delegates if and only if the OK-AS-DELEGATE flag is set in the Kerberos service ticket, which is a matter of realm policy.\nL3150: \nL3151:   * `always` - Unconditionally allow the server to delegate.\nL3152: \nL3153: http.extraHeader\nL3154: \nL3155: \nL3156: Pass an additional HTTP header when communicating with a server. If more than one such entry exists, all of them are added as extra headers. To allow overriding the settings inherited from the system config, an empty value will reset the extra headers to the empty list.\nL3157: http.cookieFile\nL3158: The pathname of a file containing previously stored cookie lines, which should be used in the Git http session, if they match the server. The file format of the file to read cookies from should be plain HTTP headers or the Netscape/Mozilla cookie file format (see `curl`(`1`)). Set it to an empty string, to accept only new cookies from the server and send them back in successive requests within same connection.\nL3159: NOTE that the file specified with http.cookieFile is used only as input unless http.saveCookies is set.\nL3160: http.saveCookies\nL3161: \nL3162: \nL3163: If set, store cookies received during requests to the file specified by http.cookieFile. Has no effect if http.cookieFile is unset, or set to an empty string.\nL3164: \nL3165: http.version\nL3166: \nL3167: \nL3168: Use the specified HTTP protocol version when communicating with a server. If you want to force the default. The available and default version depend on libcurl. Currently the possible values of this option are:\nL3169: \nL3170:   * HTTP/2\nL3171: \nL3172:   * HTTP/1.1\nL3173: \nL3174: http.curloptResolve\nL3175: Hostname resolution information that will be used first by libcurl when sending HTTP requests. This information should be in one of the following formats:\nL3176: \nL3177:   * [+]HOST:PORT:ADDRESS[,ADDRESS]\nL3178: \nL3179:   * -HOST:PORT\n"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a17e71-922f-7ef3-a2c1-bcdcbd1f8423","create_time":1793124373.421741}},"metadata":{"client_authored":false,"fallback_token_limit_override":12000}}
