{"timestamp":"2026-10-27T19:04:56.655Z","ordinal":424056,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"agent_message","id":"amsg_01a17ec0-564f-7962-8e44-90ebd353bce6","author":"/root/plan05_readonly_review","recipient":"/root","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Message Type: FINAL_ANSWER\nTask name: /root\nSender: /root/plan05_readonly_review\nPayload:\n**CHANGES_REQUIRED** — reviewed exact HEAD `a2832c01a1bb552c7d9a0ef324424a80ecb48ee3` against base `cf05322cc0368af900b2fb425852daa2d0de403e`; diff SHA-256 `5402b9842cd71134ce60daf2c9a3d99c32827bc00650922473c83557ca21db15`.\n\nThe original check-to-replacement race is handled: the regression injects a regular launcher after inspection and confirms its contents survive. The new startup recovery also handles a displaced entry when `binPath` is absent.\n\nA crash during that recovery can still strand it. Startup hard-links the displaced entry back to `binPath`, syncs, then unlinks the displaced name ([upgrade_journal.ts](/home/mike/nexus-wt/state-home-alias-backup/cli/src/upgrade_journal.ts:547)). If the process stops between those steps, the next run sees both names. The current check at lines 551–556 recognizes a completed new symlink by target, but fails to recognize that the two paths may be hard links to the same restored entry. For a regular launcher, or a restored symlink whose old target differs from the target being installed, recovery reports manual intervention and remains stuck.\n\nCheck whether `current` and `priorDisplacement` have the same device and inode before the target-based branch; if so, remove the duplicate and continue recovery. Add regression coverage for that persisted state, including a regular launcher."}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a17eb3-a9cd-72c2-8903-33ec16779f56","create_time":1793127896.6551497}},"metadata":{"client_authored":false,"user_input_order":20528}}
