{"timestamp":"2026-10-27T19:07:30.321Z","ordinal":424216,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a14cb3-25b0-7883-b3a1-0fa7cc559f92","turn_id":"01a17eb3-a9cd-72c2-8903-33ec16779f56","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-dc86099a-ff9b-4e04-8d57-e94f405c8a61","process_id":"78753","command":["/usr/bin/zsh","-lc","rg -n -i 'windows|macos|darwin|platform support|supported platforms' README.md docs/v1.0.0 cli/README.md docs 2>/dev/null | head -35; gh run view 40566060161 --repo mikeholownych/nexus-code --json jobs --jq '.jobs[] | select(.name==\"test-matrix (22)\" or .name==\"test-matrix (24)\") | \"\\(.name) status=\\(.status) current_step=\\([.steps[] | select(.status==\"in_progress\") | .name] | join(\",\"))\"' "],"cwd":"file:///home/mike/nexus-wt/state-home-alias-backup","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"unknown","cmd":"rg -n -i 'windows|macos|darwin|platform support|supported platforms' README.md docs/v1.0.0 cli/README.md docs 2>/dev/null | head -35; gh run view 40566060161 --repo mikeholownych/nexus-code --json jobs --jq '.jobs[] | select(.name==\"test-matrix (22)\" or .name==\"test-matrix (24)\") | \"\\(.name) status=\\(.status) current_step=\\([.steps[] | select(.status==\"in_progress\") | .name] | join(\",\"))\"' "}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","aggregated_output":"docs/v1.0.0/DECISIONS.md:159:**Chosen:** Linux x86-64, glibc ≥ 2.35, Node ≥ 22 for v1.0.0. The owner selected Node 22 as the minimum on 2026-10-22 because Node 20 reached end of life on 2026-04-30; v1.0.0 must not require or build on an end-of-life Node runtime. Validate Node 22 and 24 in the source CI matrix, with release and reproducibility builds on Node 22. The docs keep the conservative glibc \"≥ 2.35\" claim; the binaries reference glibc symbols ≤ 2.34. macOS, arm64 and musl are post-v1 roadmap items.\ndocs/REPOSITORY_QUALITY.md:14:Initial discovery used a GitHub App without settings-write access, an expired Linux GitHub CLI login, and an unavailable browser session. After the owner reconnected OAuth, the Windows GitHub CLI authenticated successfully as the repository administrator. The administrative settings below were then applied and verified; no additional approval was required.\ndocs/v1.0.0/AUDIT.md:373:- No macOS or other distributions.\ndocs/v1.0.0/ROADMAP.md:73:| **Post-v1** | REQUIREMENTS §5: single step loop, full context provenance, knowledge freshness, `/tdd` and `nexus eval` fixes, cgroups, macOS/arm64, self-uninstall, and others |\ndocs/v1.0.0/READINESS.md:380:  - **Distinction of evidence:** Independent public metadata review (`codex-lead`) verified and accepted the committed manifest digest (`50895b84…`), 40 unique task IDs, 10×4 class/language balance, 40 full pinned commits, and schema/digest fields without inspecting private payloads. Private payload validation (2 rounds per task in bounded `SUITE` windows: fresh clone → checkout → setup → pre-patch regression pass → pre-patch hidden fail → reference patch apply → post-patch hidden pass → post-patch regression pass), rejection/quarantine of one provenance-contaminated batch (`author-b9` at `/home/mike/nexus-heldout-v2-quarantine/b9-contaminated-2026-10-18T0235Z/`), per-session exec-log audits for clean replacement authors (`author-b8c`, `author-b9c`), and store access tightening (`/home/mike/nexus-heldout-v2` mode `0700`, `tasks/` and `reference/` mode `0500` read-only) are custodian-attested in `custody-report-2026-10-18.md`. Earlier author-exposed packages (`v12`, `v15`, `v16`, `v17`, `v18`) recorded in historical sections below are superseded development/pilot artifacts and are never used for held-out qualification.\ndocs/v1.0.0/REQUIREMENTS.md:68:| REQ-SEC-001 | RES-001..004; DEC-01 ADR | File tools are confined to the workspace (lexical, symlink, parent-symlink, `/proc/self/fd` recheck). | `file_ops.rs:59-87,731-791` | RT (C §4 R1) | tested | P | TOCTOU swap race is untested; macOS branch is a no-op | ACC-SEC-JAIL (+ race test, P) | K | — |\ndocs/v1.0.0/REQUIREMENTS.md:262:- macOS, arm64 and musl builds (DEC-12).\ndocs/v1.0.0/evidence/C_tools_security_persistence.md:123:- **macOS / case-insensitive FS.** `.GIT` and `.ENV` handling, and the non-Linux branch of `verify_descriptor_confinement`, which is a no-op (file_ops.rs:81-85). Requires a macOS CI run of `core/tests` plus new case tests.\ndocs/v1.0.0/evidence/E_distribution.md:71:| REL-13 | S3 | CI coverage | .github/workflows/ci.yml:41-66; release.yml | SOURCE + RUNTIME (CI log). There is one OS/arch (ubuntu-22.04 x86_64) and Node 20 only on GitHub; Node 22/24 is not tested, while the developer runs 24.21.0. Rust uses `dtolnay/rust-toolchain@stable`, with no `rust-toolchain.toml` and no `rust-version` in Cargo.toml, so the toolchain floats. There is no `engines` field in package.json. Nothing asserts that tracked `cli/dist` equals a build of `cli/src`. There is no musl/arm/macOS, which matches the docs. Positive: the PTY e2e suites run in CI (905 tests, 0 skipped). | Breakage on newer Node or a future stable Rust is caught late. Reproducibility depends on whatever \"stable\" is on release day. | Add a Node matrix (20, 22, 24). Pin the toolchain file and MSRV. Add an `engines` field. Add a `git diff --exit-code cli/dist` step after the build. |\ndocs/v1.0.0/DECISIONS.md:159:**Chosen:** Linux x86-64, glibc ≥ 2.35, Node ≥ 22 for v1.0.0. The owner selected Node 22 as the minimum on 2026-10-22 because Node 20 reached end of life on 2026-04-30; v1.0.0 must not require or build on an end-of-life Node runtime. Validate Node 22 and 24 in the source CI matrix, with release and reproducibility builds on Node 22. The docs keep the conservative glibc \"≥ 2.35\" claim; the binaries reference glibc symbols ≤ 2.34. macOS, arm64 and musl are post-v1 roadmap items.\ndocs/v1.0.0/READINESS.md:380:  - **Distinction of evidence:** Independent public metadata review (`codex-lead`) verified and accepted the committed manifest digest (`50895b84…`), 40 unique task IDs, 10×4 class/language balance, 40 full pinned commits, and schema/digest fields without inspecting private payloads. Private payload validation (2 rounds per task in bounded `SUITE` windows: fresh clone → checkout → setup → pre-patch regression pass → pre-patch hidden fail → reference patch apply → post-patch hidden pass → post-patch regression pass), rejection/quarantine of one provenance-contaminated batch (`author-b9` at `/home/mike/nexus-heldout-v2-quarantine/b9-contaminated-2026-10-18T0235Z/`), per-session exec-log audits for clean replacement authors (`author-b8c`, `author-b9c`), and store access tightening (`/home/mike/nexus-heldout-v2` mode `0700`, `tasks/` and `reference/` mode `0500` read-only) are custodian-attested in `custody-report-2026-10-18.md`. Earlier author-exposed packages (`v12`, `v15`, `v16`, `v17`, `v18`) recorded in historical sections below are superseded development/pilot artifacts and are never used for held-out qualification.\ndocs/v1.0.0/ROADMAP.md:73:| **Post-v1** | REQUIREMENTS §5: single step loop, full context provenance, knowledge freshness, `/tdd` and `nexus eval` fixes, cgroups, macOS/arm64, self-uninstall, and others |\ndocs/v1.0.0/REQUIREMENTS.md:68:| REQ-SEC-001 | RES-001..004; DEC-01 ADR | File tools are confined to the workspace (lexical, symlink, parent-symlink, `/proc/self/fd` recheck). | `file_ops.rs:59-87,731-791` | RT (C §4 R1) | tested | P | TOCTOU swap race is untested; macOS branch is a no-op | ACC-SEC-JAIL (+ race test, P) | K | — |\ndocs/v1.0.0/REQUIREMENTS.md:262:- macOS, arm64 and musl builds (DEC-12).\ndocs/v1.0.0/evidence/E_distribution.md:71:| REL-13 | S3 | CI coverage | .github/workflows/ci.yml:41-66; release.yml | SOURCE + RUNTIME (CI log). There is one OS/arch (ubuntu-22.04 x86_64) and Node 20 only on GitHub; Node 22/24 is not tested, while the developer runs 24.21.0. Rust uses `dtolnay/rust-toolchain@stable`, with no `rust-toolchain.toml` and no `rust-version` in Cargo.toml, so the toolchain floats. There is no `engines` field in package.json. Nothing asserts that tracked `cli/dist` equals a build of `cli/src`. There is no musl/arm/macOS, which matches the docs. Positive: the PTY e2e suites run in CI (905 tests, 0 skipped). | Breakage on newer Node or a future stable Rust is caught late. Reproducibility depends on whatever \"stable\" is on release day. | Add a Node matrix (20, 22, 24). Pin the toolchain file and MSRV. Add an `engines` field. Add a `git diff --exit-code cli/dist` step after the build. |\ndocs/v1.0.0/evidence/C_tools_security_persistence.md:123:- **macOS / case-insensitive FS.** `.GIT` and `.ENV` handling, and the non-Linux branch of `verify_descriptor_confinement`, which is a no-op (file_ops.rs:81-85). Requires a macOS CI run of `core/tests` plus new case tests.\ndocs/v1.0.0/AUDIT.md:373:- No macOS or other distributions.\ndocs/PHASE3_IMPLEMENTATION_DECOMPOSITION.md:195:4. **`ProcessLifecycleManager`:** Manages subprocess tracking, tree cancellation and termination (`SIGTERM` -> `SIGKILL` on Unix via process groups; Job Objects on Windows), process ownership, and explicit persistent daemon tracking in both Rust and TypeScript (`SUBP-001` .. `SUBP-005`, `F-06`).\ndocs/PHASE3_IMPLEMENTATION_DECOMPOSITION.md:402:  - On Windows: Utilize Windows Job Objects for robust process tree tracking and termination.\ndocs/PHASE3_IMPLEMENTATION_DECOMPOSITION.md:582:| `core/src/runner.rs` | 321 | Shell command runner in Rust | **EXTEND** | Fix `cwd` escape vulnerability (`RES-002`); implement cross-platform process tree termination abstraction (`SUBP-001` .. `SUBP-005`, `F-06`) targeting Unix process groups (`setpgid`) while supporting Windows Job Objects. |\ndocs/PHASE3_IMPLEMENTATION_DECOMPOSITION.md:612:- **Acceptance Properties:** Terminating a timed-out or cancelled command cleanly reaps all descendant child processes via platform abstraction (`setpgid` on Unix, Job Objects on Windows); zero leaked helper processes after test suite execution; persistent background processes transfer ownership cleanly.\ndocs/PHASE3_IMPLEMENTATION_DECOMPOSITION.md:866:| `SUBP-001` | CONFLICTING_EXISTING_PATH | `runner.rs` (INC-04) | Process group termination test | Process tree termination abstraction (Unix setpgid / Windows Job Objects) |\ndocs/PHASE3_IMPLEMENTATION_DECOMPOSITION.md:961:   - *Description:* True race-resistant filesystem operations (especially file creation under mutating symlinks) can vary between Linux, macOS, and Windows.\ndocs/UPGRADE_SECURITY.md:42:  - Entries containing `..` or leading `/` or Windows drive letters are rejected.\ndocs/PHASE2_REQUIREMENTS_INDEPENDENT_REVIEW.md:217:- **Why It Matters:** The underlying architectural invariant is that Nexus must own and track the lifecycle of task-spawned execution so that cancellation or timeout terminates all task-owned descendant execution. Dictating that it MUST use \"the entire process group or container\" prematurely prescribes Unix `setpgid`/`kill(-pgid)` or Linux container namespaces into the requirements contract. On other platforms (e.g., Windows Job Objects) or environments where process groups are restricted, other tracking mechanisms satisfy the lifecycle ownership invariant.\ndocs/PHASE2_REQUIREMENTS_INDEPENDENT_REVIEW.md:222:- **What Remains Implementation Choice:** Whether to implement process tracking via POSIX process groups, Linux cgroups/namespaces, Windows Job Objects, or active PID tree monitoring.\ndocs/CLOUD_PROVIDERS.md:171:- Model catalog lists are cached per provider in `~/.cache/nexus/models` and used as fallback when offline; no embedded per-model pricing metadata: set `--context-limit` for large windows.\ndocs/PHASE4_IMPLEMENTATION_WORK_PACKAGES_INDEPENDENT_REVIEW.md:317:   - *Review Disposition:* **`NORMAL VALIDATION SUFFICIENT`** is acceptable provided the adversarial process-tree termination test harness passes 100%. However, if the implementation of background daemon transfer encounters cross-platform ambiguity (e.g. differences between POSIX and Windows Job Objects), the lead coordinator should escalate `WP-03` to independent review.\ndocs/PHASE4_IMPLEMENTATION_WORK_PACKAGES.md:406:- `core/src/runner.rs`: Subprocess execution. **EXTEND** — Implement Unix process group creation (`setpgid`), graceful shutdown sequence (`SIGTERM` followed by timeout and `SIGKILL`), and Windows Job Object abstraction (`SUBP-001` .. `SUBP-003`, `F-06`). Implement environment sanitization (`CRED-002`).\ndocs/PHASE4_IMPLEMENTATION_WORK_PACKAGES.md:1483:   - *Risk:* Subprocess process groups (`setpgid`) work reliably on POSIX, while Windows requires Job Objects.\ndocs/reviews/NEXUS_TUI_SURFACE_2026-10-12.md:106:| P3-E1/E2 resume notice style and conflicting counts | `/resume` reads like the startup notice; the notice names the session, the history header carries the count | unit `narrow_windows_keep_the_mode_…_resume_reads_as_a_notice`; screens 23b, 24 |\ndocs/PHASE3_CORRECTIONS_VERIFICATION.md:120:- **Review Finding (`F-06`):** Phase 3 previously codified Unix-specific `setpgid` and negative PID signaling as normative architecture, without adequately modeling cross-platform execution or Windows Job Objects.\ndocs/PHASE3_CORRECTIONS_VERIFICATION.md:128:  - **Cross-Platform Mechanism Abstraction:** Section 10.1 defines process tree termination as a cross-platform abstraction (`UnixProcessGroup` on Linux via `libc::setpgid`; Windows Job Objects on Windows).\ndocs/PHASE3_CORRECTIONS_VERIFICATION.md:207:- **`SUBP-001` (Process Group Termination):** Note updated to process tree termination abstraction (Unix `setpgid` / Windows Job Objects).\ndocs/PHASE3_CORRECTIONS_VERIFICATION.md:240:7. **Process Lifecycle vs. Windows Modeling:** No contradiction. §10.1 and §20 preserve Linux-first implementation as the authoritative baseline while defining a portable interface abstraction.\ntest-matrix (24) status=in_progress current_step=Run cargo test --locked --manifest-path core/Cargo.toml\ntest-matrix (22) status=in_progress current_step=Run npm --prefix cli run build\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":1,"nanos":813244036}},"started_at_ms":1793128048508,"completed_at_ms":1793128050321}}
