{"timestamp":"2026-10-27T21:04:36.529Z","ordinal":427451,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a14cb3-25b0-7883-b3a1-0fa7cc559f92","turn_id":"01a17ef2-de25-7482-bff6-192772052d26","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-811effd0-f824-4513-99ac-90a33978a1fd","process_id":"10220","command":["/usr/bin/zsh","-lc","find .git/nexus-claims -maxdepth 2 -name owner.json -print0 | xargs -0 -r rg -l 'cli/src/core/(operation|capabilities)|ConvergenceScheduler|operation lifecycle|operation\\.ts|operation_runner'\ngit show origin/main:docs/v1.0.0/CONCURRENCY.md | sed -n '1,90p'\ngit show origin/main:docs/EXECUTION_CONVERGENCE.md | sed -n '1,130p'\ngit show origin/main:cli/package.json | head -80\ngit show origin/main:cli/src/core/operation.ts | wc -c\ngit show origin/main:cli/src/core/capabilities.ts | wc -c\ngit show origin/main:cli/src/core/operation_runner.ts | wc -c\n"],"cwd":"file:///home/mike/nexus-code","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"unknown","cmd":"find .git/nexus-claims -maxdepth 2 -name owner.json -print0 | xargs -0 -r rg -l 'cli/src/core/(operation|capabilities)|ConvergenceScheduler|operation lifecycle|operation\\.ts|operation_runner'\ngit show origin/main:docs/v1.0.0/CONCURRENCY.md | sed -n '1,90p'\ngit show origin/main:docs/EXECUTION_CONVERGENCE.md | sed -n '1,130p'\ngit show origin/main:cli/package.json | head -80\ngit show origin/main:cli/src/core/operation.ts | wc -c\ngit show origin/main:cli/src/core/capabilities.ts | wc -c\ngit show origin/main:cli/src/core/operation_runner.ts | wc -c\n"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","aggregated_output":"# Running multiple agents on this plan without collisions\n\nThese rules apply to every agent (Claude Code, Codex, Cursor, Nexus itself, or a human) that implements any part of this package at the same time as another. The repository's root `AGENTS.md` points here. The rules come from incidents recorded in `tasks/lessons.md`:\n\n- a concurrent agent overwrote newer evidence;\n- full test suites were contaminated by edits made mid-run;\n- `pkill -f` killed the agent's own shell;\n- HEAD moved during a pause.\n\nThey also follow from audit finding PER-03: the test suite writes into the real `~/.nexus`.\n\n## 1. Unit of work: one claimed PR slice per agent\n\n- The unit of concurrent work is **one PR slice** from a plan, for example `PLAN-01/PR-1a`. Every slice lists its tasks and its safe change boundary in its plan.\n- An agent works on exactly one claimed slice at a time.\n- An agent must not edit files outside its slice's boundary. The exceptions are the ledger rows and the shared files covered by §4. If the work needs a file outside the boundary, the agent stops and records a **boundary request** (§6). It does not edit the file.\n\n## 2. Claiming a slice (atomic and machine-local)\n\nClaims live in the repository's shared git directory. They are visible to every worktree, and they are never committed.\n\n```sh\nCLAIMS=\"$(git rev-parse --git-common-dir)/nexus-claims\"\nmkdir -p \"$CLAIMS\"\nSLICE=\"PLAN-01_PR-1a\"\nif mkdir \"$CLAIMS/$SLICE\" 2>/dev/null; then          # mkdir is atomic: exactly one agent wins\n  printf '{\"agent\":\"%s\",\"branch\":\"%s\",\"since\":\"%s\",\"pid\":%s}\\n' \\\n    \"$AGENT_NAME\" \"v1/plan-01-pr-1a\" \"$(date -u +%FT%TZ)\" \"$$\" > \"$CLAIMS/$SLICE/owner.json\"\nelse\n  echo \"claimed by: $(cat \"$CLAIMS/$SLICE/owner.json\")\"; exit 1\nfi\n```\n\n- **Release** the claim by deleting its directory (`rm -r \"$CLAIMS/$SLICE\"`) after the slice merges or is abandoned. Write one line about the outcome to `$CLAIMS/../nexus-claims.log`.\n- **Hot-file claims** use the same mechanism with the name `FILE_<path with / replaced by _>`, for example `FILE_cli_src_agent.ts`. See §4.\n- **Stale claims.** A claim older than 24 h whose PID is gone may be broken, but only by the owner, or by an agent that first records the broken claim in the log.\n- If the MCP Agent Mail server is available, its file reservations (`file_reservation_paths`) may be used as well. The git-common-dir claim stays authoritative, because every agent can see it without extra tooling.\n\n## 3. Isolation: worktree, branch, HOME, ports, processes\n\n| Resource | Rule |\n|---|---|\n| Source | One `git worktree` per claimed slice: `git worktree add ../nexus-wt/<slice> -b v1/<plan>-<slice> origin/main`. Never edit `~/nexus-code` itself. It is the owner's checkout, and `/usr/local/bin/nexus` runs from it. |\n| Branch | `v1/plan-NN-pr-Nx`. One branch per slice. Never commit to `main` directly. |\n| HOME and state | Until PLAN-08 PR-8a lands, every test, build and probe run sets `HOME=$(mktemp -d)` and `XDG_CONFIG_HOME=\"$HOME/.config\"`. After PR-8a, the test setup enforces this, but scripts and probes outside `npm test` must still set it. **Never** run anything that writes to the real `~/.nexus` or `~/.config/nexus`. |\n| Test runs | Full suites run on a **snapshot copy** of the worktree (`git worktree add` at the commit under test, or `git archive`), never on the tree being edited. `tsx` reads `src/` live. |\n| Builds | Each worktree builds into its own `core/target`, and `cli/dist` is per worktree. To share the Rust build cache, set `CARGO_TARGET_DIR` per worktree, never to one shared directory. |\n| Ports | Mock servers bind `127.0.0.1:0` (an ephemeral port) and publish the chosen port to a file. Fixed ports are forbidden. |\n| Processes | Kill only PIDs you started, taken from `$!` or `ps -eo pid,args \\| grep -v grep`. **Never `pkill -f` or `killall`.** Long background jobs are stopped through the agent's own task controls. |\n| Full test suites | The PTY and provider tests are timing-sensitive. Take the claim `SUITE` before running a full suite (`make test`, or `npm test` over all files). While anyone holds `SUITE`, nobody runs other suites or targeted test files on the same machine. Concurrent load caused a false failure on 2026-10-15: `cloud_providers` saw an extra provider request after a load-induced timeout. Release the claim when the run ends. |\n| Inference | There is one inference host (DEC-16). Any run that uses the gateway takes the claim `GATEWAY` first. That includes eval runs, live checks and stripped-proxy runs. One gateway run at a time, which keeps eval timings valid and prevents overload. Small cloud checks (E5) take `CLOUD` to avoid duplicate spend. |\n| Credentials | Agents never read or copy `~/.config/nexus/credentials.json`, `config.env` values, or gateway tokens. The stripped proxy reads the token file itself. |\n\n## 4. Hot files: serialise, do not merge blindly\n\nThese files are touched by several plans. An agent editing one must hold its `FILE_` claim for the **shortest** period: edit, test, push, merge, then release.\n\n| File | Plans that touch it | Rule |\n|---|---|---|\n| `cli/src/agent.ts` | 01, 02, 03, 04, 05, 11 | Claim required. Keep each change local to the line ranges the plan names. Rebase immediately before merge and re-run that slice's tests on the rebased snapshot. |\n| `cli/src/index.ts` | 01, 03, 04, 06, 08, 10, 11 | Claim required. **PLAN-06 PR-6b (unified launcher) lands first.** Other plans add commands and flags afterwards. |\n| `cli/src/tools.ts` | 01, 02, 03, 06, 11 | Claim required. **PLAN-02 PR-2b (signal plumbing) lands before PLAN-03 PR-3c (`safeFetch`)**. |\n| `cli/src/sidecar.ts` | 01, 02, 03, 05, 08 | Claim required |\n| `cli/src/doctor.ts` | 01, 03, 05, 06, 08 | Claim required. Each plan adds **new check functions** in separate blocks. Only PLAN-06 PR-6d changes existing checks. |\n| `cli/src/client.ts` | 03, 05, 08, 11 | Claim required. **PLAN-03 PR-3a (env layering) lands before any PLAN-05 slice.** |\n| `cli/src/repl.ts` | 01, 05, 08 | Claim required |\n| `cli/src/config.ts`, `security.ts`, `sandbox.ts`, `mcp.ts`, `plugins.ts` | 01, 03 | Claim required. PLAN-01 PR-1e and PLAN-03 must not run at the same time on `sandbox.ts` or `security.ts`. |\n| `cli/src/core/capabilities.ts` | 01, 02 | **PLAN-01 PR-1a (effect classes) lands first.** PLAN-02 PR-2c adds schema fields afterwards. |\n| `cli/src/tester.ts` | 01, 11 | **PLAN-01 PR-1c lands before PLAN-11 PR-11b/11c.** |\n| `cli/src/core/state.ts` | 04, 08 | **PLAN-08 PR-8a (HOME isolation) lands before everything.** |\n| `core/src/runner.rs`, `core/src/main.rs` | 01, 02, 06 | Claim required. The protocol version number is owned by PLAN-02 PR-2a. PLAN-06 PR-6c only reads it. |\n| `core/src/bin/nexus_tui/{live_state,live_view}.rs` | 01, 02 | Claim required |\n| `package.json`, `cli/package.json`, `core/Cargo.toml`, lockfiles | 01, 06, 07, 08 | **Only one agent at a time.** Version fields are written only by `scripts/sync-version.mjs`. Dependency changes are separate commits that touch only the lockfile and manifest. |\n| `harness.sh`, `install.sh`, `scripts/package-linux.sh`, `.github/workflows/*` | 05, 06, 07, 09, C1 | Claim required. Workflow edits are separate commits. |\n| `cli/dist/**` | all, while tracked | Do not commit `cli/dist` from slices. PLAN-07 PR-7d untracks it (DEC-10). Until that merges, only the merging agent rebuilds and commits dist, in a final separate commit, after rebasing. |\n| `docs/USER_MANUAL.md`, `ARCHITECTURE.md`, `CLOUD_PROVIDERS.md`, `quickstart.md` | 05, 09 and doc tasks in every plan | Edit only the sections your slice changes. Hold a short `FILE_` claim. |\n\n### Ledger updates (`docs/v1.0.0/REQUIREMENTS.md`)\n\nA slice changes only the rows for its own requirement IDs: the status, test-reference and evidence cells, in place. It never reorders, adds or removes rows, and never edits another slice's rows. New requirements discovered during work go to §6, not into the ledger. Because rows are independent lines, git merges these edits cleanly. If a conflict appears anyway, resolve it row by row, keeping both changes.\n\n## 5. Commit and merge discipline\n\n1. Before **every** commit, compare `git rev-parse HEAD` with the commit you last made. Then run `git log <yours>..HEAD` on the branch. If anything foreign appears, stop and reconcile. Never commit generated artifacts (evidence, dist, lockfiles) over a branch that moved (lessons 2026-10-13).\n2. Commits are scoped to the slice. Do not reformat or clean up files outside the boundary.\n3. Merge order follows the precedence rules in §4 and the ROADMAP milestone order. Within a milestone, the slice with fewer hot-file claims merges first.\n4. Rebase onto `origin/main` immediately before merging. Re-run the slice's targeted tests, plus `make test`, on a snapshot of the rebased commit. Merge only if both pass.\n5. Pushing, tagging, publishing and changing GitHub state (issues, milestones, releases) are **owner-approved actions** (DECISIONS X-list). Agents prepare them and do not perform them.\n6. Preserve unrelated user work: the untracked `nexus-continuity-validation/`, the stashes, and any dirty files you did not create. Never stash, reset, clean or check out over them.\n\n# Execution convergence: one lifecycle for autonomous work\n\nBranch `fix/inference-transport-cause`, baseline 9f103ee. Code references are to `cli/src`.\n\n```\nuser workflow/command (/team [experimental], nexus operation [experimental])\n  -> orchestration strategy (TeamOrchestrator: roles -> work units)\n  -> durable work units + dependencies (DurableOperationState, OperationStore)\n  -> convergence scheduler (ConvergenceScheduler decides; OperationRunner owns time, execution, persistence)\n  -> agent turn (AgentWorkUnitExecutor -> runTurn) / provider recovery (ProviderRecoveryController) / context admission\n  -> tools + inference resources (ResourceRegistry, LocalLlmClient)\n```\n\nInteractive turns (REPL, TUI sidecar, `nexus run`) enter at \"agent turn\" directly; see section 8.\n\n## 1. Execution-path matrix (baseline 9f103ee, before changes)\n\nRead from the code, not from earlier reports. \"Turn\" = `AutonomousAgent.runTurn`, which owns `ProviderRecoveryController`,\n`admitContext` (fail-closed capacity), truthful `TurnResult` and per-step durable checkpoints.\n\n| Path (user-visible) | Work representation | Inference | Provider recovery | Context mgmt | Success determined by | Failure propagation | WAITING | BLOCKED | Durable recovery | Explicit deps | Resource eligibility | Truthful cancel | Restart/resume |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `nexus run` / `ApplicationCore.runTask` | one turn (`agent.run`) | `runTurn` streaming | yes | yes (admission, compaction) | `TurnResult` execution/outcome | `TurnResult` → exit code / JSON | in-turn only (recovery wait) | turn `BLOCKED` (capacity) | task state per step | n/a | primary + failover endpoints | yes (signal) | `run -r <task>` |\n| REPL turn (`repl.ts`) | one turn | `runTurn` | yes | yes | `TurnResult` | rendered | in-turn only | turn `BLOCKED` | task state | n/a | primary | yes (Ctrl-C) | `/recover` |\n| Sidecar / TUI turn (`sidecar.ts`) | one turn | `runTurn` | yes | yes | `TurnResult` | turn events | in-turn only | turn `BLOCKED` | task state | n/a | primary | yes (signal) | recover command |\n| `/tdd`, `run --tdd` | several turns (synthesizer) | `runTurn` | yes | yes | verifier evidence; last turn downgraded if TDD fails | `TurnResult` | in-turn only | turn `BLOCKED` | task state | implicit phase order | primary | yes (signal) | no |\n| `nexus eval` | one turn per instance | `agent.run` | yes | yes | benchmark patch/test check | report | in-turn only | turn `BLOCKED` | task state | n/a | primary | no | no |\n| **`/team full`** (slash + legacy REPL) | 4 sequential `runSubagent` calls | `chatCompletion` (non-streaming) | **none** | `admitContext` per step | **`success: true` unless an exception escapes** | **inference error returned as report text → SUCCESS** | **no** | **no** | **none** | implicit fixed order, failure does not stop later roles | **none** (base model for every role, task-class header only) | **no** (no signal) | **no** |\n| **`/team <role>`** | one `runSubagent` | same | **none** | per step | **same false success** | same | no | no | none | n/a | none | no | no |\n| `spawn_subagent` tool | nested `runSubagent` inside a parent turn | same | **none** | per step | **tool `success: true` for any returned string**, incl. inference failure and step-limit exhaustion (historical; see §8) | same false success to the parent model | no | no | none (parent turn is durable) | n/a | parent config | no | no |\n| `nexus operation run/resume` | durable operation of work units (`OperationRunner`) | unit = `runTurn` pinned to a resource | yes (+ YIELDED to scheduler) | yes (per-resource limit) | unit `TurnResult` classified by `AgentWorkUnitExecutor.classify` | unit → BLOCKED with decision; operation BLOCKED | **yes** (durable) | **yes** | operation + per-unit task state, driver lock | yes (validated DAG) | yes (registry, conditions, capabilities, independence) | yes | yes (`resume`) |\n\nContradictions found:\n\n1. `/team` and `spawn_subagent` use a second agent loop (`runSubagent`) that bypasses provider recovery, the\n   truthful `TurnResult`, cancellation and durability, and converts inference failure into a successful report.\n2. Two \"success\" meanings: `TurnResult`/work-unit classification (evidence) vs `RoleTaskResult.success`\n   (\"the function returned\").\n3. Role → model routing exists twice: `/team` task-class headers vs scheduler requirements/resources.\n4. Credential scope: `readLatestAuthToken` (stored aihost token) is attached to any endpoint without an explicit key\n   (client constructor, 401 reload) and `buildConfig` bakes it into `config.apiKey`, which derived configs (planner,\n   coder, subagents) then send to their own endpoints. Only the scheduler path was guarded (`noAmbientCredentials`).\n\n## 2. The /team false success\n\nReproduction (`test/team_convergence.test.ts` tests 1–2, failing on 9f103ee): a provider that answers every chat\nrequest with HTTP 400. `/team researcher …` returned `success: true` with the report\n`Subagent failed during LLM inference: Inference error (HTTP 400) …`; `/team full …` ran all four roles on the\nfailed role's missing output and returned success.\n\nRoot cause: `TeamOrchestrator.executeRoleTask` built `{ success: true, output: await agent.runSubagent(...) }`, and\n`runSubagent` caught inference failure and step-limit exhaustion and *returned* them as the report string. Success\nmeant \"the function returned\". `runFullWorkflow` passed each role's text to the next role unconditionally.\n\n## 3. Lifecycle\n\nOne model for durable autonomous work, defined in `core/operation.ts` (header) and used by `nexus operation` and\n`/team`:\n\n| Unit state | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| RUNNABLE | dependencies COMPLETE; waiting for the scheduler |\n| RUNNING | a turn is executing on its assigned resource |\n| PARKED | a qualified resource will be eligible again at a known time; no model context is held |\n| WAITING_ON_DEPENDENCY | some dependency is not COMPLETE (a BLOCKED dependency keeps it here) |\n| COMPLETE | the turn completed with an established result: `turnSucceeded(TurnResult)`, the same predicate as `nexus run` exit 0 (outcome `VERIFIED`, or `NOT_REQUIRED`). `UNVERIFIED` work is not complete |\n| BLOCKED | no safe autonomous transition; carries one structured decision |\n\n| Operation state | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| RUNNING | some unit is runnable or running (the \"active\" state) |\n| WAITING | nothing can run now; required work is parked on a known time |\n| BLOCKED | nothing can run or wait; required work needs a decision |\n| COMPLETE | every required unit is COMPLETE |\n| CANCELLED | the operator stopped it; history preserved |\n\nThere is no FAILED state. Terminal execution failure (provider rejection, step limit, unknown execution with its\nreplay budget spent, interrupted tool, no qualified resource) is BLOCKED with a decision; environmental conditions\n(rate limit, capacity, transport outage) park or reroute. `TurnResult` remains the per-turn vocabulary; the unit\nstate is derived from it in `AgentWorkUnitExecutor.classify`, so no second set of execution enums exists. A turn\nthat completed without an established result (`UNVERIFIED`: verification not run, no applicable verifier, or\n`CONTEXT_EVIDENCE_NOT_RETAINED`) is BLOCKED with a retry/revise decision, never COMPLETE.\n\n`NOT_REQUIRED` means the turn changed nothing *and* every tool output it relied on was still in its context. When\ncontext compaction removes a tool observation (elided or truncated by the detail budget) and the turn does not run\nthat tool with the same arguments again, the turn is `UNVERIFIED` with reason `CONTEXT_EVIDENCE_NOT_RETAINED`. The\ndurable record tells the model exactly which outputs left its context (`Output no longer in context (rerun to see it\nagain): <tool> <args>`), carries which notes still hold full output (`retainedObservations`), and a resumed unit\nre-derives what it no longer holds from its recovered conversation. Removal tracking is per turn (plus resumed\nconversations); an interactive REPL turn relying on output compacted away in an earlier turn is not flagged.\n\n### Decisions\n\nA BLOCKED unit carries `{ id: \"<unit>#<n>\", cause, question, options[] }`. Every option is a typed `DecisionAction`:\n`retry` (optionally clearing operator-needed resource conditions), `keep_waiting`, `assign` (to a named resource,\nlisting the requirements it overrides — never independence or trust), `revise` (operator note appended to the unit's\ntask), `drop` (advisory units only) and `cancel_operation`. `ConvergenceScheduler.resolve` validates the decision id,\nthe option id and the action against current state and throws `DecisionRejected` otherwise; applying one records\nit on `unit.decisions`, emits `DECISION_APPLIED` and resumes the operation under the driver lock. Operators apply\nthem with `nexus operation decide <operation> <decision> <option> [--note …] [--no-resume]`. Nothing else mutates a\nBLOCKED unit.\n\n## 4. /team on the convergence foundation\n\n`TeamOrchestrator` is now a strategy: it maps the request to work units and drives them through `driveOperation`\n(the same entry `nexus operation run/resume` uses). `executeRoleTask`, `runFullWorkflow`, `RoleTaskResult` and the\nrole-specific `runSubagent` prompt plumbing are removed.\n\n| Unit | Role | Depends on | Requirement |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| research | researcher | — | qualification `research`, capability `tools` |\n| design | architect | research | `architecture` |\n| implement | implementer | design | `implementation` |\n| verify | tester | implement | `verification` |\n| review (only with `--independent-review`, or `/team reviewer`) | reviewer | implement | `review`, `independentOf: [implement]` |\n\n`/team <role>` is a one-unit operation. Each unit is an `AgentWorkUnitExecutor` turn (streaming `runTurn` with\nprovider recovery, context admission and a durable task conversation) run in 15-step segments (with durable checkpoints at segment boundaries; under default continuation units run until completion, stagnation or cancellation, while `--no-continuation` bounds the turn at 15 steps); dependency summaries are\npassed to dependents as context. The operation is persisted under `~/.nexus/tasks/team-xxxxxxxx.json`, so\n`nexus operation status|resume|decide` work on team operations. `/team` waits in-process up to 60 s; a longer wait\nreturns `NOT COMPLETE (WAITING)` with the resume command. Ctrl-C in the legacy REPL aborts the operation\n(CANCELLED, resumable). The report is derived from operation state: `Result: COMPLETE` only when the operation is\nCOMPLETE.\n\nRoles are defined once in `core/roles.ts` (`ROLE_PROFILES`, `ROLE_REQUIREMENTS`).\n\n## 5. Capability routing and independence\n\nResources come from configuration (`resourcesFromConfig`): primary, failover endpoints, and the planner/coder models\nwhen configured. The planner is `preferredFor` research/architecture/review and the coder for\n{\n  \"name\": \"local-code-harness\",\n  \"version\": \"1.0.0-rc.10\",\n  \"type\": \"module\",\n  \"description\": \"Autonomous local AI coding harness (Codex / Claude-Code style) for LM Studio and vLLM\",\n  \"main\": \"dist/index.js\",\n  \"bin\": {\n    \"harness\": \"./dist/index.js\"\n  },\n  \"scripts\": {\n    \"build\": \"tsc && node scripts/write_build_info.mjs\",\n    \"test\": \"node scripts/run_tests.mjs && bash ../scripts/terminal-acceptance/test-scope-procs.sh\",\n    \"start\": \"node dist/index.js\",\n    \"dev\": \"tsx watch src/index.ts\"\n  },\n  \"keywords\": [\n    \"ai\",\n    \"coding-agent\",\n    \"codex\",\n    \"claude-code\",\n    \"vllm\",\n    \"lm-studio\",\n    \"rust\"\n  ],\n  \"author\": \"\",\n  \"license\": \"MIT\",\n  \"engines\": {\n    \"node\": \">=22\"\n  },\n  \"dependencies\": {\n    \"commander\": \"^12.1.0\",\n    \"dotenv\": \"^16.4.5\",\n    \"ora\": \"^8.1.0\",\n    \"picocolors\": \"^1.1.1\"\n  },\n  \"devDependencies\": {\n    \"@types/node\": \"^22.5.0\",\n    \"tsx\": \"^4.19.0\",\n    \"typescript\": \"^5.5.4\"\n  }\n}\n35527\n45099\n42503\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":30084}},"started_at_ms":1793135076528,"completed_at_ms":1793135076529}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-27T21:04:37.940Z","ordinal":427452,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a17f2d-ea34-76b1-a63b-ae998dad9088","call_id":"call_5f7d7a5e4f774a8b8172413238ac0110","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 2.6 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"Warning: truncated output (original token count: 5261)\nTotal output lines: 264\n\n# Running multiple agents on this plan without collisions\n\nThese rules apply to every agent (Claude Code, Codex, Cursor, Nexus itself, or a human) that implements any part of this package at the same time as another. The repository's root `AGENTS.md` points here. The rules come from incidents recorded in `tasks/lessons.md`:\n\n- a concurrent agent overwrote newer evidence;\n- full test suites were contaminated by edits made mid-run;\n- `pkill -f` killed the agent's own shell;\n- HEAD moved during a pause.\n\nThey also follow from audit finding PER-03: the test suite writes into the real `~/.nexus`.\n\n## 1. Unit of work: one claimed PR slice per agent\n\n- The unit of concurrent work is **one PR slice** from a plan, for example `PLAN-01/PR-1a`. Every slice lists its tasks and its safe change boundary in its plan.\n- An agent works on exactly one claimed slice at a time.\n- An agent must not edit files outside its slice's boundary. The exceptions are the ledger rows and the shared files covered by §4. If the work needs a file outside the boundary, the agent stops and records a **boundary request** (§6). It does not edit the file.\n\n## 2. Claiming a slice (atomic and machine-local)\n\nClaims live in the repository's shared git directory. They are visible to every worktree, and they are never committed.\n\n```sh\nCLAIMS=\"$(git rev-parse --git-common-dir)/nexus-claims\"\nmkdir -p \"$CLAIMS\"\nSLICE=\"PLAN-01_PR-1a\"\nif mkdir \"$CLAIMS/$SLICE\" 2>/dev/null; then          # mkdir is atomic: exactly one agent wins\n  printf '{\"agent\":\"%s\",\"branch\":\"%s\",\"since\":\"%s\",\"pid\":%s}\\n' \\\n    \"$AGENT_NAME\" \"v1/plan-01-pr-1a\" \"$(date -u +%FT%TZ)\" \"$$\" > \"$CLAIMS/$SLICE/owner.json\"\nelse\n  echo \"claimed by: $(cat \"$CLAIMS/$SLICE/owner.json\")\"; exit 1\nfi\n```\n\n- **Release** the claim by deleting its directory (`rm -r \"$CLAIMS/$SLICE\"`) after the slice merges or is abandoned. Write one line about the outcome to `$CLAIMS/../nexus-claims.log`.\n- **Hot-file claims** use the same mechanism with the name `FILE_<path with / replaced by _>`, for example `FILE_cli_src_agent.ts`. See §4.\n- **Stale claims.** A claim older than 24 h whose PID is gone may be broken, but only by the owner, or by an agent that first records the broken claim in the log.\n- If the MCP Agent Mail server is available, its file reservations (`file_reservation_paths`) may be used as well. The git-common-dir claim stays authoritative, because every agent can see it without extra tooling.\n\n## 3. Isolation: worktree, branch, HOME, ports, processes\n\n| Resource | Rule |\n|---|---|\n| Source | One `git worktree` per claimed slice: `git worktree add ../nexus-wt/<slice> -b v1/<plan>-<slice> origin/main`. Never edit `~/nexus-code` itself. It is the owner's checkout, and `/usr/local/bin/nexus` runs from it. |\n| Branch | `v1/plan-NN-pr-Nx`. One branch per slice. Never commit to `main` directly. |\n| HOME and state | Until PLAN-08 PR-8a lands, every test, build and probe run sets `HOME=$(mktemp -d)` and `XDG_CONFIG_HOME=\"$HOME/.config\"`. After PR-8a, the test setup enforces this, but scripts and probes outside `npm test` must still set it. **Never** run anything that writes to the real `~/.nexus` or `~/.config/nexus`. |\n| Test runs | Full suites run on a **snapshot copy** of the worktree (`git worktree add` at the commit under test, or `git archive`), never on the tree being edited. `tsx` reads `src/` live. |\n| Builds | Each worktree builds into its own `core/target`, and `cli/dist` is per worktree. To share the Rust build cache, set `CARGO_TARGET_DIR` per worktree, never to one shared directory. |\n| Ports | Mock servers bind `127.0.0.1:0` (an ephemeral port) and publish the chosen port to a file. Fixed ports are forbidden. |\n| Processes | Kill only PIDs you started, taken from `$!` or `ps -eo pid,args \\| grep -v grep`. **Never `pkill -f` or `killall`.** Long background jobs are stopped through the agent's own task controls. |\n| Full test suites | The PTY and provider tests are timing-sensitive. Take the claim `SUITE` before running a full suite (`make test`, or `npm test` over all files). While anyone holds `SUITE`, nobody runs other suites or targeted test files on the same machine. Concurrent load caused a false failure on 2026-10-15: `cloud_providers` saw an extra provider request after a load-induced timeout. Release the claim when the run ends. |\n| Inference | There is one inference host (DEC-16). Any run that uses the gateway takes the claim `GATEWAY` first. That includes eval runs, live checks and stripped-proxy runs. One gateway run at a time, which keeps eval timings valid and prevents overload. Small cloud checks (E5) take `CLOUD` to avoid duplicate spend. |\n| Credentials | Agents never read or copy `~/.config/nexus/credentials.json`, `config.env` values, or gateway tokens. The stripped proxy reads the token file itself. |\n\n## 4. Hot files: serialise, do not merge blindly\n\nThese files are touched by several plans. An agent editing one must hold its `FILE_` claim for the **shortest** period: edit…2761 tokens truncated…onditions\n(rate limit, capacity, transport outage) park or reroute. `TurnResult` remains the per-turn vocabulary; the unit\nstate is derived from it in `AgentWorkUnitExecutor.classify`, so no second set of execution enums exists. A turn\nthat completed without an established result (`UNVERIFIED`: verification not run, no applicable verifier, or\n`CONTEXT_EVIDENCE_NOT_RETAINED`) is BLOCKED with a retry/revise decision, never COMPLETE.\n\n`NOT_REQUIRED` means the turn changed nothing *and* every tool output it relied on was still in its context. When\ncontext compaction removes a tool observation (elided or truncated by the detail budget) and the turn does not run\nthat tool with the same arguments again, the turn is `UNVERIFIED` with reason `CONTEXT_EVIDENCE_NOT_RETAINED`. The\ndurable record tells the model exactly which outputs left its context (`Output no longer in context (rerun to see it\nagain): <tool> <args>`), carries which notes still hold full output (`retainedObservations`), and a resumed unit\nre-derives what it no longer holds from its recovered conversation. Removal tracking is per turn (plus resumed\nconversations); an interactive REPL turn relying on output compacted away in an earlier turn is not flagged.\n\n### Decisions\n\nA BLOCKED unit carries `{ id: \"<unit>#<n>\", cause, question, options[] }`. Every option is a typed `DecisionAction`:\n`retry` (optionally clearing operator-needed resource conditions), `keep_waiting`, `assign` (to a named resource,\nlisting the requirements it overrides — never independence or trust), `revise` (operator note appended to the unit's\ntask), `drop` (advisory units only) and `cancel_operation`. `ConvergenceScheduler.resolve` validates the decision id,\nthe option id and the action against current state and throws `DecisionRejected` otherwise; applying one records\nit on `unit.decisions`, emits `DECISION_APPLIED` and resumes the operation under the driver lock. Operators apply\nthem with `nexus operation decide <operation> <decision> <option> [--note …] [--no-resume]`. Nothing else mutates a\nBLOCKED unit.\n\n## 4. /team on the convergence foundation\n\n`TeamOrchestrator` is now a strategy: it maps the request to work units and drives them through `driveOperation`\n(the same entry `nexus operation run/resume` uses). `executeRoleTask`, `runFullWorkflow`, `RoleTaskResult` and the\nrole-specific `runSubagent` prompt plumbing are removed.\n\n| Unit | Role | Depends on | Requirement |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| research | researcher | — | qualification `research`, capability `tools` |\n| design | architect | research | `architecture` |\n| implement | implementer | design | `implementation` |\n| verify | tester | implement | `verification` |\n| review (only with `--independent-review`, or `/team reviewer`) | reviewer | implement | `review`, `independentOf: [implement]` |\n\n`/team <role>` is a one-unit operation. Each unit is an `AgentWorkUnitExecutor` turn (streaming `runTurn` with\nprovider recovery, context admission and a durable task conversation) run in 15-step segments (with durable checkpoints at segment boundaries; under default continuation units run until completion, stagnation or cancellation, while `--no-continuation` bounds the turn at 15 steps); dependency summaries are\npassed to dependents as context. The operation is persisted under `~/.nexus/tasks/team-xxxxxxxx.json`, so\n`nexus operation status|resume|decide` work on team operations. `/team` waits in-process up to 60 s; a longer wait\nreturns `NOT COMPLETE (WAITING)` with the resume command. Ctrl-C in the legacy REPL aborts the operation\n(CANCELLED, resumable). The report is derived from operation state: `Result: COMPLETE` only when the operation is\nCOMPLETE.\n\nRoles are defined once in `core/roles.ts` (`ROLE_PROFILES`, `ROLE_REQUIREMENTS`).\n\n## 5. Capability routing and independence\n\nResources come from configuration (`resourcesFromConfig`): primary, failover endpoints, and the planner/coder models\nwhen configured. The planner is `preferredFor` research/architecture/review and the coder for\n{\n  \"name\": \"local-code-harness\",\n  \"version\": \"1.0.0-rc.10\",\n  \"type\": \"module\",\n  \"description\": \"Autonomous local AI coding harness (Codex / Claude-Code style) for LM Studio and vLLM\",\n  \"main\": \"dist/index.js\",\n  \"bin\": {\n    \"harness\": \"./dist/index.js\"\n  },\n  \"scripts\": {\n    \"build\": \"tsc && node scripts/write_build_info.mjs\",\n    \"test\": \"node scripts/run_tests.mjs && bash ../scripts/terminal-acceptance/test-scope-procs.sh\",\n    \"start\": \"node dist/index.js\",\n    \"dev\": \"tsx watch src/index.ts\"\n  },\n  \"keywords\": [\n    \"ai\",\n    \"coding-agent\",\n    \"codex\",\n    \"claude-code\",\n    \"vllm\",\n    \"lm-studio\",\n    \"rust\"\n  ],\n  \"author\": \"\",\n  \"license\": \"MIT\",\n  \"engines\": {\n    \"node\": \">=22\"\n  },\n  \"dependencies\": {\n    \"commander\": \"^12.1.0\",\n    \"dotenv\": \"^16.4.5\",\n    \"ora\": \"^8.1.0\",\n    \"picocolors\": \"^1.1.1\"\n  },\n  \"devDependencies\": {\n    \"@types/node\": \"^22.5.0\",\n    \"tsx\": \"^4.19.0\",\n    \"typescript\": \"^5.5.4\"\n  }\n}\n35527\n45099\n42503\n"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a17ef2-de25-7482-bff6-192772052d26","create_time":1793135077.9403985}},"metadata":{"client_authored":false,"fallback_token_limit_override":12000}}
